1
00:00:55,139 --> 00:00:57,725
<i>Obviously,
I miscalculated a few things.</i>

2
00:01:04,566 --> 00:01:08,736
Eddie! I know you're in there!

3
00:01:14,492 --> 00:01:17,954
<i>Why is it that the moment your life
exceeds your wildest dreams...</i>

4
00:01:18,037 --> 00:01:19,581
<i>The access code you have entered...</i>

5
00:01:19,664 --> 00:01:21,958
<i>...the knife appears at your back?</i>

6
00:01:22,041 --> 00:01:25,336
<i>The access code
you have entered is invalid...</i>

7
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:28,840
<i>Well, I'll tell you one thing...</i>

8
00:01:51,029 --> 00:01:53,448
<i>...I will never let them touch me.</i>

9
00:02:06,461 --> 00:02:09,172
<i>My neighbor, who must have
opened his door to complain.</i>

10
00:02:09,255 --> 00:02:12,759
You're gonna be next, Eddie!
We're coming in!

11
00:02:14,677 --> 00:02:18,598
<i>For a guy with a four digit IQ,
I must have missed something.</i>

12
00:02:18,681 --> 00:02:20,558
<i>And I hadn't missed much.</i>

13
00:02:21,976 --> 00:02:24,520
<i>I'd come this close to having
an impact on the world.</i>

14
00:02:25,897 --> 00:02:28,441
<i>And now, the only thing
I'd have an impact on...</i>

15
00:02:29,609 --> 00:02:31,235
<i>...was the sidewalk.</i>

16
00:03:53,192 --> 00:03:54,902
<i>You see that guy?</i>

17
00:03:54,986 --> 00:03:56,988
<i>That was me, not so long ago.</i>

18
00:03:57,905 --> 00:04:00,992
<i>What kind of guy without a drug or
alcohol problem looks this way?</i>

19
00:04:01,701 --> 00:04:03,786
<i>Only a writer. Strangely enough,</i>

20
00:04:03,870 --> 00:04:06,539
<i>nobody believed that
I actually had a book contract.</i>

21
00:04:06,622 --> 00:04:08,332
Well, it's masquerading
as a sci-fi novel,

22
00:04:08,416 --> 00:04:10,084
but it's really, uh,
my own personal manifesto

23
00:04:10,168 --> 00:04:12,086
about the plight of the
individual in the 21st century.

24
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:14,422
I sort of created a utopian society

25
00:04:14,505 --> 00:04:16,716
where we all sort of...
Uh... Uh...

26
00:04:16,799 --> 00:04:19,510
It's really... it's really...

27
00:04:23,306 --> 00:04:24,265
Uh...

28
00:04:26,642 --> 00:04:29,771
<i>Today was the day
I was gonna kick its ass.</i>

29
00:04:29,854 --> 00:04:32,857
<i>Here we go.
Here we go. Here we go...</i>

30
00:04:34,317 --> 00:04:36,027
<i>It was gonna be great.</i>

31
00:04:36,569 --> 00:04:39,322
<i>Just gotta put in the time.
Stay in the room.</i>

32
00:04:40,823 --> 00:04:42,116
<i>That's the key concept.</i>

33
00:04:43,367 --> 00:04:45,661
<i>Don't leave the room.</i>

34
00:04:53,127 --> 00:04:55,713
<i>A few weeks passed this way.</i>

35
00:04:55,796 --> 00:04:57,131
<i>Maybe a few months.</i>

36
00:05:03,095 --> 00:05:04,263
<i>At least I still had Lindy.</i>

37
00:05:07,558 --> 00:05:09,101
Just like that?

38
00:05:10,228 --> 00:05:14,565
Come on, Eddie.
This is hardly a surprise.

39
00:05:15,608 --> 00:05:17,068
I'm surprised.

40
00:05:18,903 --> 00:05:20,321
Let's just not do this, OK?
Let me...

41
00:05:20,404 --> 00:05:22,114
I'm gonna hand in
90 pages to Marla on Friday.

42
00:05:22,198 --> 00:05:23,658
If you just wait to see
what she says...

43
00:05:23,741 --> 00:05:24,951
Eddie.

44
00:05:25,034 --> 00:05:27,245
- What?
- I know how it's going.

45
00:05:28,037 --> 00:05:29,080
I'm your...

46
00:05:29,914 --> 00:05:32,124
I was your girlfriend.

47
00:05:33,251 --> 00:05:35,628
That word doesn't even begin
to describe what you are to me.

48
00:05:35,711 --> 00:05:39,090
Partner? Squeeze?

49
00:05:39,173 --> 00:05:42,051
Paramour. Inamorata.

50
00:05:42,134 --> 00:05:45,346
Cleaning lady. Bank.

51
00:05:48,891 --> 00:05:50,268
Be that a...

52
00:05:51,310 --> 00:05:52,645
What's mine is yours, too,

53
00:05:52,728 --> 00:05:55,147
and you know I want to share
the rest of my life with you.

54
00:05:55,231 --> 00:05:57,400
- I was thinking that we should...
- Don't propose.

55
00:05:58,317 --> 00:05:59,318
Why not?

56
00:06:00,361 --> 00:06:03,447
Because it worked out
so well for you last time.

57
00:06:03,531 --> 00:06:06,659
<i>It's true, I did marry once.
Right out of college, to Melissa.</i>

58
00:06:06,742 --> 00:06:08,327
I do.

59
00:06:08,411 --> 00:06:09,412
<i>Briefly.</i>

60
00:06:09,495 --> 00:06:11,289
- This isn't working.

61
00:06:16,294 --> 00:06:18,629
Well, even though everything you
say is true, I still love you.

62
00:06:24,552 --> 00:06:26,512
I gotta get back to work.

63
00:06:28,764 --> 00:06:30,725
You didn't even
tell me what happened.

64
00:06:31,475 --> 00:06:33,144
- I got it.
- You did?

65
00:06:33,227 --> 00:06:35,521
Mmm-hm. I'm an editor.

66
00:06:35,605 --> 00:06:38,024
I'll have my own assistant.
Can you believe that?

67
00:06:38,858 --> 00:06:40,735
Yeah, of course I believe it.
You deserve it.

68
00:06:43,154 --> 00:06:44,280
Thanks.

69
00:06:47,450 --> 00:06:48,784
- OK.
- OK.

70
00:06:56,334 --> 00:06:58,461
<i>She was right. Why stick it out?</i>

71
00:06:59,295 --> 00:07:01,422
<i>I had clearly missed the on-ramp.</i>

72
00:07:02,089 --> 00:07:03,883
<i>We both knew what was beckoning.</i>

73
00:07:03,966 --> 00:07:07,219
<i>The lower bunk in my
childhood bedroom in Jersey.</i>

74
00:07:07,303 --> 00:07:09,889
<i>My father happy to we/come me
into the challenging field</i>

75
00:07:09,972 --> 00:07:12,058
<i>of dental supply inventory.</i>

76
00:07:12,683 --> 00:07:13,893
Eddie Morra.

77
00:07:15,978 --> 00:07:18,230
<i>Of all the useless
relationships better forgotten</i>

78
00:07:18,314 --> 00:07:19,857
<i>and put away in mothballs,</i>

79
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:22,902
<i>is there any more useless than...
the ex-brother-in-law?</i>

80
00:07:22,985 --> 00:07:25,237
Holy shit, man.

81
00:07:25,321 --> 00:07:27,239
What has it been? Like nine years?

82
00:07:27,323 --> 00:07:28,574
- God!
- Hey.

83
00:07:29,659 --> 00:07:32,244
You all right? You look like
you're living on the streets.

84
00:07:32,328 --> 00:07:33,329
What's going on?

85
00:07:34,288 --> 00:07:36,540
Not much, I... Well...
What am I... What's up?

86
00:07:36,624 --> 00:07:38,709
Uh, I'm writing. Writing a lot.

87
00:07:38,793 --> 00:07:40,419
Oh, you're still trying to write?

88
00:07:41,212 --> 00:07:42,713
Matter of fact,
I have a book contract.

89
00:07:43,464 --> 00:07:44,590
- Really?
- Yeah.

90
00:07:44,674 --> 00:07:46,175
- That's great.
- How about you?

91
00:07:46,258 --> 00:07:47,677
Are you still dealing, Vern?

92
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:51,055
- Do I look like I'm still dealing?
- No, you don't. No.

93
00:07:51,555 --> 00:07:53,391
Come on, let's go get a drink.
I want to hear about this book.

94
00:07:54,183 --> 00:07:56,394
I don't know.
It's 2:00 in the afternoon.

95
00:07:56,977 --> 00:07:59,939
- When has that ever stopped you?

96
00:08:02,233 --> 00:08:04,777
- So.
- So...

97
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:06,487
How's Melissa doing?

98
00:08:06,570 --> 00:08:08,239
- Here you go.
- Thanks.

99
00:08:08,322 --> 00:08:12,451
Wouldn't know. I don't see her.
She moved upstate.

100
00:08:12,535 --> 00:08:17,123
She got some kind of Internet home sales
kind of job. Couple kids.

101
00:08:17,206 --> 00:08:20,167
Couple kids. Who's the husband?

102
00:08:21,168 --> 00:08:24,755
He walked out on her,
if you really want to know.

103
00:08:24,839 --> 00:08:26,257
But what do you care, man?

104
00:08:27,049 --> 00:08:29,510
You were married, like, five minutes.

105
00:08:29,593 --> 00:08:32,513
Look, I don't wanna talk about her.
I wanna talk about you, OK?

106
00:08:32,596 --> 00:08:35,391
Tell me about this book.
How's it going?

107
00:08:36,892 --> 00:08:38,644
Well, it's, uh...

108
00:08:38,728 --> 00:08:40,104
I'm... I'm...

109
00:08:41,230 --> 00:08:43,023
I'm behind.
I'm behind on my book.

110
00:08:43,107 --> 00:08:47,361
And, uh, it's pretty well polluting my days
and nights, if you really want to know.

111
00:08:47,445 --> 00:08:50,906
- Well, how much have you written of it?
- Not one word.

112
00:08:50,990 --> 00:08:54,410
- Creative problems, huh?
- Yeah. Mm-hm.

113
00:08:57,329 --> 00:09:00,291
Well, I suppose
I can help you with that.

114
00:09:00,374 --> 00:09:02,084
Just this once.

115
00:09:03,002 --> 00:09:04,712
Oh, no, no, no, no, no.

116
00:09:04,795 --> 00:09:08,299
- You don't even know what it is.
- You're still dealing.

117
00:09:08,382 --> 00:09:10,968
Brother,
I am light years from that now.

118
00:09:11,051 --> 00:09:15,181
I've been doing, uh, some consulting
for a pharmaceutical company.

119
00:09:15,264 --> 00:09:18,017
What, like some offshore lab
making fake Viagra? Come on, Vern.

120
00:09:18,100 --> 00:09:22,938
No. This is an exclusive product
that's coming on stream next year.

121
00:09:23,022 --> 00:09:25,900
They've had clinical trials
and it's FDA approved.

122
00:09:26,692 --> 00:09:28,903
All right, just out of curiosity
and that's all...

123
00:09:30,821 --> 00:09:32,239
Let's see it.

124
00:09:35,326 --> 00:09:36,368
What's in it?

125
00:09:37,077 --> 00:09:39,288
They've identified these receptors
in the brain

126
00:09:39,371 --> 00:09:42,166
that activate specific circuits.

127
00:09:42,249 --> 00:09:45,920
And you how they say that we can
only access 20 percent of our brain?

128
00:09:46,587 --> 00:09:51,091
Well, what this does...
it lets you access all of it.

129
00:09:52,384 --> 00:09:55,721
Vern, look at me. Do I look good to you?
I'm broke and I'm depressed off my ass.

130
00:09:55,805 --> 00:09:58,349
I don't think that my life's
gonna take some sudden upswing

131
00:09:58,432 --> 00:10:01,685
into fame and fortune by taking some shiny,
brand-new designer drug.

132
00:10:01,769 --> 00:10:04,855
Gant. When?

133
00:10:04,939 --> 00:10:08,984
Tell him we can't do that.
No, you tell him.

134
00:10:10,820 --> 00:10:12,029
No, now.

135
00:10:13,405 --> 00:10:17,243
Well, I'm afraid I'm gonna
have to leave you here, Eddie.

136
00:10:17,326 --> 00:10:19,995
But I really... I really do
want to do this again.

137
00:10:20,079 --> 00:10:23,374
So, call me.
And, uh, that's on the house.

138
00:10:24,250 --> 00:10:25,668
I don't want it.

139
00:10:26,252 --> 00:10:28,963
Don't be ungrateful.
You know how much that costs?

140
00:10:29,046 --> 00:10:31,674
Eight hundred bucks. A pop.

141
00:10:32,967 --> 00:10:33,926
You're welcome.

142
00:10:45,479 --> 00:10:47,523
<i>All the way home,
I thought about Melissa.</i>

143
00:10:48,274 --> 00:10:50,943
<i>How could she be a failure, too?
She'd been so smart.</i>

144
00:10:51,819 --> 00:10:54,905
<i>- Smarter than anyone around her.</i>

145
00:10:54,989 --> 00:10:57,366
<i>Clearly, there was a direct link
between this Eddie,</i>

146
00:10:57,449 --> 00:11:00,536
<i>slightly drunk at 3:00 in the afternoon,
and an earlier Eddie...</i>

147
00:11:01,704 --> 00:11:04,665
<i>...vomiting on his boss's desk
or stealing his dying aunt's Percocet.</i>

148
00:11:06,375 --> 00:11:09,420
<i>So who could blame Lindy
for dumping me?</i>

149
00:11:18,304 --> 00:11:21,390
<i>In the end, how much worse could it get?</i>

150
00:11:29,773 --> 00:11:31,775
<i>I didn't want to see anybody.</i>

151
00:11:31,859 --> 00:11:34,695
<i>Especially not my landlord's
nasty young wife.</i>

152
00:11:34,778 --> 00:11:37,114
- Valerie, Tuesday.
- Look. Enough, OK?

153
00:11:37,197 --> 00:11:39,491
Steve handles the rent,
so you can feed your crap to him.

154
00:11:39,575 --> 00:11:41,493
<i>I suddenly had extra reason
to get away from her.</i>

155
00:11:41,577 --> 00:11:44,371
<i>I had thoughtlessly ingested
a substance.</i>

156
00:11:44,455 --> 00:11:45,831
Like the rent's not low enough!

157
00:11:45,915 --> 00:11:48,584
<i>I had gotten remarkably
little information from Vernon</i>

158
00:11:48,667 --> 00:11:50,377
<i>about what this drug would do.</i>

159
00:11:50,461 --> 00:11:54,048
You could even be a bike messenger
and come up with that. Really.

160
00:11:54,131 --> 00:11:56,425
<i>- What if it was a hallucinogen?
- It's pathetic.</i>

161
00:11:56,508 --> 00:11:58,886
<i>Oh, my God.
Listening to her rag...</i>

162
00:11:58,969 --> 00:12:00,638
<i>If I were tripping,
I'd jump out a fucking window.</i>

163
00:12:00,721 --> 00:12:02,890
Hello? Are you listening to me?

164
00:12:02,973 --> 00:12:05,976
Do you get it?
You are gonna be out on the streets!

165
00:12:06,060 --> 00:12:07,811
<i>And then, I felt it.</i>

166
00:12:38,884 --> 00:12:41,095
<i>I was blind, but now I see.</i>

167
00:12:56,110 --> 00:12:58,654
...running away!
You can't even look at me, you know why?

168
00:12:58,737 --> 00:13:00,948
Because you're not even a man.
You're not even a man!

169
00:13:01,031 --> 00:13:04,410
You don't have rent!
You don't have a job!

170
00:13:15,754 --> 00:13:17,881
- What's wrong?
- What?

171
00:13:17,965 --> 00:13:20,426
You don't like me and I don't blame you.
You see a self-defeating,

172
00:13:20,509 --> 00:13:23,220
energy-sucking piece of shit
who's sponging off your husband.

173
00:13:23,303 --> 00:13:24,638
You're wishing I'd blow my brains out,

174
00:13:24,722 --> 00:13:27,641
but my existence shouldn't make you
this upset. What is it?

175
00:13:28,642 --> 00:13:31,353
- That's none of your business.
- Something wrong at law school?

176
00:13:31,437 --> 00:13:32,813
How do you know I'm in law school?

177
00:13:32,896 --> 00:13:34,523
People who aren't, usually
don't carry around dry,

178
00:13:34,606 --> 00:13:37,151
academically-constipated books
about a dead Supreme Court justice.

179
00:13:37,860 --> 00:13:40,195
You're a creep, aren't you?
You've been... You've been following me.

180
00:13:40,279 --> 00:13:43,323
- No, I just noticed the book.
- You just saw the corner of it.

181
00:13:43,407 --> 00:13:44,783
How did you know that?

182
00:13:44,867 --> 00:13:47,953
<i>I'd seen it before.
Twelve years ago in college.</i>

183
00:13:48,037 --> 00:13:50,289
<i>Sitting on the couch of a TA
I was trying to make,</i>

184
00:13:50,372 --> 00:13:52,124
<i>waiting for her to come
back out of the bathroom,</i>

185
00:13:52,207 --> 00:13:54,168
<i>hoping she'd have a condom.</i>

186
00:13:54,251 --> 00:13:56,795
<i>Somehow my unconscious
had served that up.</i>

187
00:13:56,879 --> 00:13:58,422
<i>A memory I had never even recorded.</i>

188
00:13:58,505 --> 00:14:02,217
<i>Or was it there the whole time
and all I needed was the access?</i>

189
00:14:02,301 --> 00:14:04,428
If you're writing a paper,
that's not the book I'd use.

190
00:14:04,511 --> 00:14:06,305
Well, who asked you?

191
00:14:06,388 --> 00:14:08,307
Hastings has his oral history.
I'd start there.

192
00:14:08,390 --> 00:14:10,476
Interesting point.
Grammatically, this guy was an idiot,

193
00:14:10,559 --> 00:14:11,894
which sort of gives credence
to the theory

194
00:14:11,977 --> 00:14:12,978
that one of the clerks he had fired

195
00:14:13,062 --> 00:14:15,189
actually wrote most of
this guy's major opinions.

196
00:14:15,272 --> 00:14:17,608
You could Google the clerk's sons,
they'd love to talk to you.

197
00:14:17,691 --> 00:14:20,360
Exonerate their dad. That'd give you
something that no one else has...

198
00:14:20,444 --> 00:14:23,197
<i>Information from the odd
museum show, a half-read article,</i>

199
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:26,325
<i>some PBS documentary... it was all
bubbling up in my frontal lobes,</i>

200
00:14:26,408 --> 00:14:29,828
<i>mixing itself together into a sparkling
cocktail of useful information.</i>

201
00:14:31,914 --> 00:14:34,750
<i>- She didn't have a chance.</i>
- So, what are your suggestions?

202
00:14:39,630 --> 00:14:41,840
<i>We'd really worked
on her paper, too.</i>

203
00:14:42,633 --> 00:14:46,261
<i>In 45 minutes, it was a polished gem.
She was pleased.</i>

204
00:14:52,476 --> 00:14:56,271
<i>Home. But it couldn't be my home,
could it?</i>

205
00:14:57,105 --> 00:14:58,565
<i>Who would live like this?</i>

206
00:15:00,192 --> 00:15:02,611
<i>Torch it.</i>

207
00:15:03,821 --> 00:15:05,447
<i>But saner heads prevailed.</i>

208
00:15:40,899 --> 00:15:43,235
<i>What was this drug?
I couldn't stay messy on it,</i>

209
00:15:43,318 --> 00:15:48,156
<i>I hadn't had a cigarette in six hours,
hadn't eaten, so... abstemious and tidy?</i>

210
00:15:48,240 --> 00:15:51,702
<i>What was this? A drug for people who
wanted to be more anal retentive?</i>

211
00:15:51,785 --> 00:15:54,580
<i>I wasn't high, I wasn't wired.
Just clear.</i>

212
00:15:54,663 --> 00:15:57,165
<i>I knew what I needed to do
and how to do it.</i>

213
00:16:34,953 --> 00:16:40,375
<i>The next morning, I sent
a little probe down into my brain.</i>

214
00:16:40,459 --> 00:16:44,046
<i>No surge of brilliance
came up to greet me.</i>

215
00:16:47,424 --> 00:16:50,677
<i>In short... I was back.</i>

216
00:16:55,349 --> 00:16:57,267
<i>But, something remained.</i>

217
00:17:01,396 --> 00:17:02,397
You're kidding.

218
00:17:03,357 --> 00:17:06,485
- No.
- Words have appeared on paper.

219
00:17:06,568 --> 00:17:09,529
- Yes.
- Written by you.

220
00:17:09,613 --> 00:17:10,989
All you have to do is read three pages.

221
00:17:11,073 --> 00:17:14,159
Just read three pages
in the next hour and...

222
00:17:14,242 --> 00:17:16,787
...if you don't want to keep reading,
I'll give you back the advance.

223
00:17:20,415 --> 00:17:21,917
OK.

224
00:17:23,293 --> 00:17:25,504
- OK, Eddie.
- OK.

225
00:17:52,489 --> 00:17:54,449
<i>Eddie,
give me a call when you get in.</i>

226
00:17:56,535 --> 00:18:01,164
<i>Eddie, I'm 40 pages further in.
Call me.</i>

227
00:18:01,248 --> 00:18:04,376
<i>It's a little grandiose,
but I'm still reading.</i>

228
00:18:04,459 --> 00:18:08,213
<i>OK, how did you do this?</i>

229
00:18:08,296 --> 00:18:13,468
<i>I'd really like... Just call me
the minute you get in. Call. OK?</i>

230
00:18:29,484 --> 00:18:32,738
- Who is it?
- Hey, Vernon, it's Eddie.

231
00:18:32,821 --> 00:18:37,325
- Eddie who?
- Uh, Morra.

232
00:18:39,578 --> 00:18:41,747
It's not a good time, Eddie.

233
00:18:41,830 --> 00:18:45,000
Uh, hey, Vern, I really
just need to talk to you.

234
00:18:46,460 --> 00:18:48,170
Hey... Oh...

235
00:18:51,214 --> 00:18:54,843
- Vern, what happened?
- Don't worry about it. I'm fine.

236
00:18:55,886 --> 00:18:58,805
So, Eddie.
You are interested after all.

237
00:18:58,889 --> 00:19:02,476
- That stuff's amazing.
- Works better if you're already smart.

238
00:19:03,477 --> 00:19:05,187
Vern, who did this to you?

239
00:19:05,729 --> 00:19:07,189
Trust me, you don't want to know.

240
00:19:08,857 --> 00:19:11,610
Uh...

241
00:19:11,693 --> 00:19:15,739
- So, uh, what's it called?
- Doesn't have a street name yet.

242
00:19:15,822 --> 00:19:19,618
But the boys in the kitchen
are calling it NZT 48.

243
00:19:19,701 --> 00:19:22,913
"The boys in the kitchen"?
I don't...

244
00:19:22,996 --> 00:19:26,374
That doesn't sound
very FDA-approved, Vern.

245
00:19:26,458 --> 00:19:31,129
FDA-approved, that's a laugh. You didn't
really believe that shit, did ya?

246
00:19:32,547 --> 00:19:34,132
<i>OK, so what do we have here?</i>

247
00:19:34,216 --> 00:19:37,385
<i>Some unknown, untested,
possibly dangerous drug</i>

248
00:19:37,469 --> 00:19:39,805
<i>scammed out of some
unidentified lab somewhere</i>

249
00:19:39,888 --> 00:19:44,267
<i>given to me by a highly unreliable guy
I hadn't seen in years.</i>

250
00:19:44,351 --> 00:19:47,229
- So you want some more?
- Yes. Definitely.

251
00:19:49,231 --> 00:19:53,151
All right, we'll talk about it,
but I, uh, need a favor first.

252
00:19:53,235 --> 00:19:56,446
Obviously, I'm in no condition
to go out right now, so,

253
00:19:56,530 --> 00:20:02,160
I was wondering if you could pop down to
the dry cleaners, grab my suit for me.

254
00:20:02,244 --> 00:20:06,623
And maybe grab a little...
a little breakfast for us.

255
00:20:08,583 --> 00:20:09,876
<i>He knew he had me.</i>

256
00:20:10,627 --> 00:20:11,628
Sure.

257
00:20:12,796 --> 00:20:16,550
<i>From now on,
I'd happily be his errand boy.</i>

258
00:20:16,633 --> 00:20:19,928
<i>Wash his windows,
scrub his toilet...</i>

259
00:20:20,011 --> 00:20:22,764
<i>...anything I could do to get my hands
on that little clear pill</i>

260
00:20:22,848 --> 00:20:25,934
<i>that would bring back...
Enhanced Eddie.</i>

261
00:20:37,404 --> 00:20:38,947
Vern?

262
00:20:49,082 --> 00:20:50,834
Vern?

263
00:21:13,481 --> 00:21:14,900
Vern...

264
00:21:17,527 --> 00:21:19,654
on, Vern.

265
00:21:41,801 --> 00:21:44,095
Hey, hey!

266
00:21:44,804 --> 00:21:46,306
Hey!

267
00:21:57,817 --> 00:22:02,280
<i>- 911, what's your emergency?</i>
- Hi, I need to report a...

268
00:22:02,364 --> 00:22:03,823
...a murder.

269
00:22:07,118 --> 00:22:08,745
7B, yes.

270
00:22:11,539 --> 00:22:13,041
OK, I won't.

271
00:22:15,543 --> 00:22:20,131
<i>Took them forever. And the longer
I stood there, the clearer I saw.</i>

272
00:22:20,215 --> 00:22:22,342
<i>Vern had known whoever had done this.</i>

273
00:22:23,593 --> 00:22:26,263
<i>And one guess what
they'd been looking for.</i>

274
00:22:30,850 --> 00:22:32,602
<i>But had they found it?</i>

275
00:23:12,017 --> 00:23:15,145
Shit, if you ever cooked,
I'd be dead, too.

276
00:23:49,346 --> 00:23:52,474
Police Department!
Open the door, please!

277
00:23:55,018 --> 00:23:57,896
Police Department! Open the door!

278
00:24:01,983 --> 00:24:06,363
Open the door, please!

279
00:24:07,781 --> 00:24:09,908
Police! Open the door!

280
00:24:09,991 --> 00:24:12,911
Let's go! Open the door!

281
00:24:12,994 --> 00:24:14,287
- Let me see your hands.
- Hey.

282
00:24:14,371 --> 00:24:16,331
- Let me see your hands!
- No, no, I'm the one who called!

283
00:24:16,414 --> 00:24:17,832
I called you guys!

284
00:24:20,960 --> 00:24:23,755
So, you guys tight?
Hang out a lot?

285
00:24:23,838 --> 00:24:27,634
No, my ex-wife's brother. I just
actually ran into him on the street

286
00:24:27,717 --> 00:24:30,261
- and he asked me to come up to...
- Buy some drugs?

287
00:24:30,345 --> 00:24:34,224
- No, no. What?
- What did this guy do?

288
00:24:34,307 --> 00:24:37,602
He... Uh, I don't know. He was, um...

289
00:24:37,685 --> 00:24:40,105
I heard that he was an antiques dealer?

290
00:24:40,188 --> 00:24:44,025
- A dealer?
- Yeah, uh, antiques. Um...

291
00:24:44,109 --> 00:24:47,195
Like, uh, Viennese kinda, um...

292
00:24:48,113 --> 00:24:49,989
- ...chairs and stuff.

293
00:24:50,073 --> 00:24:52,784
Like, you know,
like curlicue leg things.

294
00:24:52,867 --> 00:24:53,952
Yeah?

295
00:24:55,036 --> 00:24:57,997
That is correct. An Edward Morra.

296
00:24:58,081 --> 00:24:59,416
He's here.

297
00:25:00,375 --> 00:25:02,043
Victim's sister.

298
00:25:02,836 --> 00:25:05,630
<i>I hadn't heard her voice
in ten years.</i>

299
00:25:06,381 --> 00:25:09,092
<i>- Eddie?</i>
- Melissa?

300
00:25:09,801 --> 00:25:12,720
<i>- You were there?</i>
- Um...

301
00:25:12,804 --> 00:25:17,892
Just before. I had run into him
on the street and, uh... yeah.

302
00:25:17,976 --> 00:25:23,189
<i>Oh, God. I wish I was more surprised.
He was involved in some stuff.</i>

303
00:25:24,023 --> 00:25:25,483
<i>I'd better not say any more.</i>

304
00:25:25,567 --> 00:25:28,736
No, no, that's probably a good idea.

305
00:25:28,820 --> 00:25:33,366
So, uh... We should probably, uh,
meet or something, right?

306
00:25:33,450 --> 00:25:38,163
<i>No, I gotta do the funeral
and God knows what else. I just...</i>

307
00:25:38,246 --> 00:25:40,457
<i>We can't meet, OK?</i>

308
00:25:40,540 --> 00:25:42,876
Right. Well, I'll just... I guess I'll
just see you at the funeral then.

309
00:25:42,959 --> 00:25:46,296
<i>No. I don't want that. Please
don't. I'll call you at some point, OK?</i>

310
00:25:46,379 --> 00:25:49,382
<i>- When this is over.</i>
- OK, all right.

311
00:25:49,466 --> 00:25:50,675
<i>OK.</i>

312
00:25:55,305 --> 00:25:59,851
You know, something doesn't gel here.

313
00:25:59,934 --> 00:26:03,563
Write down your number.
I may need to contact you later.

314
00:26:36,054 --> 00:26:38,473
<i>Worth the risk?</i>

315
00:26:43,811 --> 00:26:45,730
<i>What would you do?</i>

316
00:27:15,843 --> 00:27:20,014
<i>Vern's cash, combined with
an unprecedented surge of motivation,</i>

317
00:27:20,098 --> 00:27:22,892
<i>enabled me to finish the book
in four days.</i>

318
00:27:33,486 --> 00:27:34,946
<i>A tablet a day</i>

319
00:27:35,029 --> 00:27:37,448
<i>and what I could do
with my day was limitless.</i>

320
00:27:38,616 --> 00:27:40,910
<i>I learned to play
the piano in three days.</i>

321
00:27:43,246 --> 00:27:44,789
<i>Math became useful.</i>

322
00:27:45,707 --> 00:27:46,791
<i>And fun.</i>

323
00:27:46,874 --> 00:27:48,459
I'm all in.

324
00:27:52,755 --> 00:27:55,717
<i>Even half-listening
to any language, I became fluent.</i>

325
00:28:03,266 --> 00:28:05,768
So that Shakespeare's catchiness
belies mediocrity?

326
00:28:06,603 --> 00:28:07,604
There's no arguing with you.

327
00:28:09,939 --> 00:28:12,233
<i>This was great.</i>

328
00:28:12,317 --> 00:28:15,028
So, Mom, I've been doing a little
research on Aunt Helen's tumor.

329
00:28:15,111 --> 00:28:18,698
And it's totally clear that anybody with
familial adenomatous polyposis

330
00:28:18,781 --> 00:28:20,992
should be supplementing
platinum-based doublet regiments

331
00:28:21,075 --> 00:28:22,452
with eicosapentaeonic acid.

332
00:28:22,535 --> 00:28:25,079
<i>I suddenly knew everything.
About everything.</i>

333
00:28:25,163 --> 00:28:27,665
Well, sure, you get a short-term spike,
but wouldn't that rapid expansion

334
00:28:27,749 --> 00:28:29,500
devalue the stock completely
in two years?

335
00:28:29,584 --> 00:28:32,086
- No, because there are safeguards.
- Against aggressive over-expansion?

336
00:28:32,170 --> 00:28:34,505
There aren't. Because there are
no safeguards in human nature.

337
00:28:34,589 --> 00:28:36,090
We're wired to overreach.

338
00:28:36,174 --> 00:28:38,384
Look at history, all the countries
that ever ruled the world.

339
00:28:38,468 --> 00:28:39,969
Portugal with this big massive navy.

340
00:28:40,053 --> 00:28:41,846
All they got now are salt cod
and cheap condoms.

341
00:28:41,929 --> 00:28:42,889
- And the Brits?

342
00:28:42,972 --> 00:28:45,058
Now they're just sitting on a dank
little island fussing over their suits.

343
00:28:45,141 --> 00:28:46,809
No one's stopping and thinking,
"Hey, we're doing pretty well."

344
00:28:46,893 --> 00:28:49,312
We got France, we got Poland,
we got a big Swiss bank account.

345
00:28:49,395 --> 00:28:51,064
You know what?
Let's not invade Russia in the winter.

346
00:28:51,147 --> 00:28:53,941
"Let's go home, let's pop a beer,
and let's live off the interest."

347
00:28:54,025 --> 00:28:55,443
- Yeah.

348
00:28:56,277 --> 00:28:57,904
<i>My brain was just
pouring this stuff out.</i>

349
00:28:57,987 --> 00:29:01,658
<i>Everything I had ever read, heard,
seen was now organized and available.</i>

350
00:29:01,741 --> 00:29:03,242
<i>Here it is. Here you go.</i>

351
00:29:03,868 --> 00:29:06,663
Eddie. Hey. Listen,
I'm sure you have a portfolio,

352
00:29:06,746 --> 00:29:09,957
but if you don't, I'd be very
interested in working with you.

353
00:29:10,041 --> 00:29:12,418
OK, Kevin.

354
00:29:12,919 --> 00:29:15,713
<i>I made some new friends
who invited me to the beach.</i>

355
00:29:15,797 --> 00:29:18,132
<i>The beach was not nearby.</i>

356
00:29:30,520 --> 00:29:33,189
<i>All my fear,
all my shyness, gone.</i>

357
00:29:33,272 --> 00:29:35,149
They've done four basic trials...

358
00:29:36,109 --> 00:29:38,653
<i>But mere lounging wasn't enough.</i>

359
00:30:03,845 --> 00:30:06,848
<i>Only problem?
If I wasn't moving forward,</i>

360
00:30:06,931 --> 00:30:09,183
<i>I felt like I was going to explode.</i>

361
00:30:09,267 --> 00:30:11,394
Come up here because
it's beautiful, right?

362
00:30:11,477 --> 00:30:14,355
- Anybody ever jump?
- Are you crazy?

363
00:30:18,818 --> 00:30:20,778
- He's gonna do it.
- He's not gonna do it.

364
00:30:22,822 --> 00:30:23,865
Oh, my God!

365
00:30:36,043 --> 00:30:37,545
Oh!

366
00:30:39,255 --> 00:30:40,423
Ah!

367
00:30:40,506 --> 00:30:44,552
<i>And then I began to form an idea.</i>

368
00:30:44,635 --> 00:30:47,054
<i>Suddenly, I knew exactly
what I needed to do.</i>

369
00:30:47,138 --> 00:30:49,724
<i>It wasn't writing.
It wasn't books.</i>

370
00:30:49,807 --> 00:30:51,851
<i>It was much bigger than that.</i>

371
00:30:54,604 --> 00:30:56,481
<i>But it would take money to get there.</i>

372
00:30:59,442 --> 00:31:02,028
<i>In a down market,
no one was making much.</i>

373
00:31:03,196 --> 00:31:05,531
<i>But no one had NZT.</i>

374
00:31:05,615 --> 00:31:08,743
<i>In an optimistic scenario,
gains of that nature merely would reflect</i>

375
00:31:08,826 --> 00:31:11,704
<i>growth in the economy
and would not pose an obstacle...</i>

376
00:31:18,294 --> 00:31:23,549
<i>Armed with Vern's last $800
I made 2,000 in a day.</i>

377
00:31:23,633 --> 00:31:25,968
<i>Next day, 7, 500.</i>

378
00:31:26,052 --> 00:31:29,430
<i>It was too slow.
I'd need more capital.</i>

379
00:31:30,181 --> 00:31:32,475
<i>But banks weren't lending
to guys like me.</i>

380
00:31:32,558 --> 00:31:33,518
Hey, Liev.

381
00:31:33,601 --> 00:31:36,103
That's your man
in the corner back there.

382
00:31:40,233 --> 00:31:41,442
Yeah.

383
00:31:49,617 --> 00:31:54,288
So, uh, Liev told you that
I'm looking for a short-term loan?

384
00:31:54,372 --> 00:31:56,290
Yeah, and I told Liev forget it.

385
00:31:56,832 --> 00:31:57,833
Why?

386
00:31:57,917 --> 00:32:02,004
Because I don't see you before
and I don't fucking like you already.

387
00:32:02,088 --> 00:32:05,049
Why do I give you $100,000?

388
00:32:05,341 --> 00:32:06,676
Because...

389
00:32:08,553 --> 00:32:12,265
...I quintupled my money
four days in a row.

390
00:32:12,348 --> 00:32:14,267
So you got a fix on the game.

391
00:32:14,350 --> 00:32:16,102
No, not a fix.

392
00:32:16,185 --> 00:32:18,604
I was able to calculate certain patterns
using algorithms.

393
00:32:18,688 --> 00:32:21,107
If you look at column four,
this was the first...

394
00:32:23,526 --> 00:32:26,529
You're good.

395
00:32:29,156 --> 00:32:30,324
OK, it's a fix.

396
00:32:30,408 --> 00:32:33,286
Yeah, you know,
all you people get caught.

397
00:32:33,369 --> 00:32:34,662
What are you gonna do then?

398
00:32:35,496 --> 00:32:37,957
Well, it's not as if I have
a black book with your name in it,

399
00:32:38,040 --> 00:32:39,792
if that's what you're talking about.

400
00:32:39,875 --> 00:32:42,128
And besides, I get the strong feeling

401
00:32:42,211 --> 00:32:45,089
that you're not somebody
I want to disappoint.

402
00:32:50,136 --> 00:32:51,721
Don't get up.
I said don't get up.

403
00:32:51,804 --> 00:32:53,139
- Sit down!
- Hey. OK.

404
00:32:55,141 --> 00:32:58,352
- OK, you take this, you mine.
- OK.

405
00:32:58,436 --> 00:33:02,064
You understand?
You don't pay, you know what we do?

406
00:33:02,148 --> 00:33:04,191
I cut you at waist.

407
00:33:04,275 --> 00:33:08,029
Peel your skin up over your head
and tie knot in it.

408
00:33:08,112 --> 00:33:10,531
You don't die from this.
You suffocate.

409
00:33:10,615 --> 00:33:12,950
- OK.
- Good luck.

410
00:33:20,916 --> 00:33:23,711
<i>My new friend, Kevin Doyle,
showed me how you could leverage</i>

411
00:33:23,794 --> 00:33:27,715
<i>two and a half times your cash
at the day trading firm.</i>

412
00:33:27,798 --> 00:33:29,634
<i>I'd been upping the dose
for over a week.</i>

413
00:33:31,927 --> 00:33:34,513
<i>It seemed to cut my /earning curve.</i>

414
00:33:48,986 --> 00:33:51,781
Why are you buying that?
The CEO was just indicted.

415
00:33:51,864 --> 00:33:54,700
But not for the big fat defense contract
he bribed his way into.

416
00:33:54,784 --> 00:33:57,203
That's still on.
Should be announced next week.

417
00:33:57,286 --> 00:34:00,164
<i>I was jacked in. Booted up.</i>

418
00:34:00,247 --> 00:34:02,041
<i>At the end of the week,
my brokerage account</i>

419
00:34:02,124 --> 00:34:04,168
<i>contained over two million dollars.</i>

420
00:34:05,169 --> 00:34:07,088
<i>Word quickly got out.</i>

421
00:34:12,968 --> 00:34:15,930
<i>You have 19 messages.</i>

422
00:34:16,013 --> 00:34:18,516
<i>Four job offers,
my bank raising my line of credit,</i>

423
00:34:18,599 --> 00:34:20,267
<i>a reporter from the New York Post.</i>

424
00:34:20,351 --> 00:34:22,269
<i>Really call me back, Mr. Morra.</i>

425
00:34:22,353 --> 00:34:23,771
<i>No one makes money that fast.</i>

426
00:34:23,854 --> 00:34:26,315
<i>This article's gonna get written
with or without you.</i>

427
00:34:26,399 --> 00:34:29,527
<i>And finally,
Kevin Doyle trying to sound casual.</i>

428
00:34:29,610 --> 00:34:34,031
<i>Eddie, I was talking you up to my boss
and, well, the impossible happened, bud.</i>

429
00:34:34,115 --> 00:34:36,951
<i>We got you a meeting with Carl Van Loon.</i>

430
00:34:37,034 --> 00:34:39,495
Carl Van Loon wants to meet you?

431
00:34:39,578 --> 00:34:43,249
- Apparently so.
- But you're not in the finance game.

432
00:34:43,332 --> 00:34:45,960
What can you do for Carl Van Loon?

433
00:35:06,313 --> 00:35:07,898
Since when do you speak Italian?

434
00:35:08,524 --> 00:35:11,485
Oh.
Self-improvement month.

435
00:35:11,569 --> 00:35:13,696
I guess somebody
gave me a wake up call.

436
00:35:14,530 --> 00:35:15,948
I hope you didn't do it for me.

437
00:35:16,031 --> 00:35:18,159
No, I just wanted
to apologize to you, Lin.

438
00:35:19,410 --> 00:35:22,955
And let you know that apparently
my capacity for self-sabotage

439
00:35:23,038 --> 00:35:25,040
wasn't boundless after all.

440
00:35:26,751 --> 00:35:29,086
The real question is,
why did you ever put up with me?

441
00:35:30,504 --> 00:35:32,173
I was in love with you.

442
00:35:38,929 --> 00:35:40,473
So, when does the book come out?

443
00:35:40,556 --> 00:35:42,433
Next year.
How's your new assistant?

444
00:35:42,516 --> 00:35:44,185
- She's good.
- Good.

445
00:35:44,268 --> 00:35:46,604
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

446
00:35:49,106 --> 00:35:51,108
And, uh, are you gonna get a new...

447
00:35:51,192 --> 00:35:54,320
You know...
I'm really happy you're here with me.

448
00:35:57,406 --> 00:36:01,452
I'm proud of you. And...

449
00:36:07,416 --> 00:36:09,168
...a little intimidated.

450
00:36:10,669 --> 00:36:12,797
<i>We started up again.</i>

451
00:36:16,091 --> 00:36:17,384
<i>Her place.</i>

452
00:36:21,430 --> 00:36:22,598
<i>My place.</i>

453
00:36:25,184 --> 00:36:26,644
<i>Basically every place.</i>

454
00:36:30,356 --> 00:36:32,525
"In a sizzling
three days of trading,"

455
00:36:32,608 --> 00:36:35,402
"the unknown Mr. Morra
trumped the market,"

456
00:36:35,486 --> 00:36:38,364
"prompting one top, hedge
fund manager to comment""

457
00:36:38,447 --> 00:36:41,408
"He's Houdini.
He's a prophet of our times."'

458
00:36:41,492 --> 00:36:42,827
OK, give me that.
Give me that!

459
00:36:42,910 --> 00:36:45,120
- "He's God"?
- You coming over later?

460
00:36:45,704 --> 00:36:48,040
I can't. I don't have a key.

461
00:36:48,123 --> 00:36:49,375
Oh, that's right.

462
00:36:50,000 --> 00:36:53,504
- Can I have it back?
- Uh... Nope.

463
00:36:53,587 --> 00:36:55,422
- No?
- I mean, does it have to be that key?

464
00:36:55,506 --> 00:36:57,341
Can it be a new key?

465
00:36:58,467 --> 00:37:00,219
To our place?

466
00:37:01,971 --> 00:37:05,891
- It would be cost effective.
- And you say I'm not practical.

467
00:37:17,528 --> 00:37:19,196
Can I walk you to work?

468
00:37:36,505 --> 00:37:38,257
Whatcha doing, hon?

469
00:37:39,133 --> 00:37:40,801
Nothing.

470
00:37:41,927 --> 00:37:43,804
You should sleep.

471
00:37:46,640 --> 00:37:48,517
Isn't your Van Loon meeting tomorrow?

472
00:38:03,449 --> 00:38:05,993
Eddie?

473
00:38:06,076 --> 00:38:07,912
Are you OK?

474
00:38:10,164 --> 00:38:12,291
What are you doing out here?

475
00:38:16,503 --> 00:38:18,923
When's the last time you ate something?

476
00:38:26,472 --> 00:38:30,267
There he is.
Nice article in the Post, buddy.

477
00:38:32,269 --> 00:38:34,939
OK, he is on his way.

478
00:38:35,648 --> 00:38:38,317
Now, be warned. He's mercurial.

479
00:38:38,734 --> 00:38:41,737
One minute, your best friend,
the next, you're a leper.

480
00:38:41,820 --> 00:38:45,950
And he needs direct answers. Anything
tentative and you've lost him forever.

481
00:38:48,035 --> 00:38:49,703
I think we should rehearse
a few scenarios.

482
00:38:50,287 --> 00:38:51,538
I'm eating, Kevin.

483
00:38:52,081 --> 00:38:53,749
Hey, are you up for this?

484
00:38:55,000 --> 00:38:57,002
You sure? 'Cause I got
a little bit on the line here.

485
00:38:57,086 --> 00:38:59,171
Have a toast point.

486
00:39:01,799 --> 00:39:03,717
- It's Carl Van Loon.
- That's Carl Van Loon.

487
00:39:03,801 --> 00:39:04,802
He's worth a fortune.

488
00:39:04,885 --> 00:39:08,055
- Hey, John.
- Hey, Carl. Good to see you.

489
00:39:08,138 --> 00:39:09,723
- How are ya?
- Eddie.

490
00:39:16,814 --> 00:39:19,233
So, Eddie Morra.

491
00:39:19,316 --> 00:39:22,361
- What's your secret?
- Medication.

492
00:39:22,444 --> 00:39:24,154
I'm on special medication.

493
00:39:24,697 --> 00:39:26,198
OK.

494
00:39:28,993 --> 00:39:30,744
Well, it's a formula
with a complex method.

495
00:39:30,828 --> 00:39:33,247
But, of course, research was
a component. Massive research.

496
00:39:33,330 --> 00:39:36,375
I knew every helix of every scrap of DNA
in every company I bought.

497
00:39:36,458 --> 00:39:39,086
Papers stacked like the Himalayas
in my office, and you know what?

498
00:39:39,169 --> 00:39:41,296
- The stocks went up.
- By a hair.

499
00:39:41,380 --> 00:39:44,550
Sure, the stocks rose,
but who wants to do all that work

500
00:39:44,633 --> 00:39:46,176
for a shitty little uptake?

501
00:39:46,260 --> 00:39:49,430
It's not the visceral kind of spike
you want in short term holdings so...

502
00:39:49,513 --> 00:39:51,181
...I dispensed
with all the hard statistics

503
00:39:51,265 --> 00:39:52,558
and I went right to the rumor mill.

504
00:39:52,641 --> 00:39:55,769
So you bought your stocks based on what?
The tom-toms beating in the street?

505
00:39:55,853 --> 00:39:59,356
Sure. Share prices aren't really
based on how a company works.

506
00:39:59,440 --> 00:40:00,941
It's about how mass psychology works.

507
00:40:01,025 --> 00:40:03,235
So, if I found certain algorithms
in the perception of the stock...

508
00:40:03,318 --> 00:40:05,612
Pattern recognition.
That's your snake oil.

509
00:40:05,696 --> 00:40:07,990
Well, not everybody
understands the patterns.

510
00:40:08,073 --> 00:40:10,784
And that gave me a little bit more of
an uptake, but it's just a parlor trick.

511
00:40:10,868 --> 00:40:12,453
So then, I went on
to door number three.

512
00:40:12,536 --> 00:40:15,873
Are you really implying that you have
some sort of ultimate formula?

513
00:40:15,956 --> 00:40:19,209
Well, from 12,000 to
2.3 million in ten days.

514
00:40:19,293 --> 00:40:22,755
- I do have a formula, Mr. Pierce.
- Delusions of grandeur.

515
00:40:22,838 --> 00:40:27,051
I don't have delusions of grandeur,
I have an actual recipe for grandeur.

516
00:40:30,012 --> 00:40:33,140
I don't know who you are, Eddie, or what
you game is, but I'm sure of one thing.

517
00:40:33,223 --> 00:40:34,600
You don't work in my world.

518
00:40:34,683 --> 00:40:38,103
I'm up to my ass with investment guys
and you don't have their half-terrified,

519
00:40:38,187 --> 00:40:40,230
half-cocky line of crap which is...

520
00:40:40,314 --> 00:40:43,025
...not to say that
I like yours any better.

521
00:40:43,108 --> 00:40:46,070
But you obviously pick your stocks
in a way which I haven't seen before.

522
00:40:46,153 --> 00:40:50,074
Take a look at these companies
and tell me what you think.

523
00:40:50,157 --> 00:40:52,534
- Right now?
- Yeah. Take your time.

524
00:40:54,995 --> 00:40:56,413
Sure.

525
00:41:09,259 --> 00:41:11,929
These companies aren't the question,
are they?

526
00:41:13,388 --> 00:41:14,640
Explain.

527
00:41:14,723 --> 00:41:18,268
Unless you're playing
both sides of the fence.

528
00:41:18,352 --> 00:41:20,187
Control the whole power enchilada,
but, no,

529
00:41:20,270 --> 00:41:22,731
these companies wouldn't get you there
in emerging markets.

530
00:41:22,815 --> 00:41:24,858
You would have to... Oh. Whoa.

531
00:41:24,942 --> 00:41:28,612
This must be some big-ass merger
you're contemplating.

532
00:41:30,781 --> 00:41:32,574
And there's really only one company
with enough bling

533
00:41:32,658 --> 00:41:34,660
to justify a merger
with Van Loon and Associates.

534
00:41:35,953 --> 00:41:37,663
Who have you been talking to?

535
00:41:37,746 --> 00:41:41,375
Oh, I'm just connecting the dots.
Is Hank Atwood really gonna go for it?

536
00:41:41,458 --> 00:41:45,003
Well, you're an amazing con artist,
or you're a very smart young man.

537
00:41:45,087 --> 00:41:47,798
I mean, come on.
The two of you together?

538
00:41:47,881 --> 00:41:49,424
The whole world would have
to come begging for energy

539
00:41:49,508 --> 00:41:51,552
like Oliver Twist and his bowl of gruel.

540
00:41:51,635 --> 00:41:54,138
Now you're going backwards.
What else you got?

541
00:41:54,221 --> 00:41:58,475
- A prediction. It won't come off.
- Why's that?

542
00:41:58,559 --> 00:42:01,937
- You'd have to back off Libya.
- Really?

543
00:42:03,147 --> 00:42:06,483
Well, you don't want to hear this from
some schmuck who needs a ride home.

544
00:42:06,567 --> 00:42:10,237
All right, you get your shot. Meet me
tomorrow at the Saint Regis at ten

545
00:42:10,320 --> 00:42:12,573
and tell me how the gutsy little schmuck
who needs a ride home

546
00:42:12,656 --> 00:42:15,909
would re-structure these deals.
And you'd better be prepared.

547
00:42:15,993 --> 00:42:17,494
I'm at your disposal.

548
00:42:17,578 --> 00:42:19,580
You don't really live here,
do you?

549
00:42:19,663 --> 00:42:21,874
Well... Spartans weren't
big on amenities.

550
00:42:22,541 --> 00:42:25,169
Yeah, and they eventually
got their asses kicked.

551
00:42:32,426 --> 00:42:37,764
<i>I didn't go in. I wanted
to walk, move, digest, ingest.</i>

552
00:42:37,848 --> 00:42:41,143
<i>There are moments in life... moments
when you know you've crossed a bridge.</i>

553
00:42:41,226 --> 00:42:42,561
<i>Your old life is over.</i>

554
00:42:42,644 --> 00:42:45,522
<i>Van Loon was my bridge.
I finally had my shot.</i>

555
00:42:45,606 --> 00:42:49,109
<i>Wall Street would provide my nest egg,
but then how far could I go?</i>

556
00:42:49,193 --> 00:42:52,905
<i>CEO? A global force?
Maybe President.</i>

557
00:42:52,988 --> 00:42:55,741
<i>Time somebody shook up the free world
and got things done.</i>

558
00:43:07,794 --> 00:43:09,546
<i>Suddenly...</i>

559
00:43:11,381 --> 00:43:16,220
<i>It was another skip.
How had I gone the last 20 blocks?</i>

560
00:43:18,430 --> 00:43:22,684
<i>I got another ten blocks.
And then...</i>

561
00:43:25,062 --> 00:43:27,189
<i>...I was way uptown.</i>

562
00:44:10,482 --> 00:44:12,818
<i>What bar was this?</i>

563
00:44:25,455 --> 00:44:28,959
...black canvas and then they would
just drop all the paint...

564
00:44:31,086 --> 00:44:33,505
Yeah. Did you ever meet...

565
00:44:34,631 --> 00:44:37,217
...and then I actually went
there and had lunch, I went back.

566
00:44:37,300 --> 00:44:38,343
My friends are rolling...

567
00:44:40,595 --> 00:44:42,514
<i>I heard you like to party.</i>

568
00:45:06,621 --> 00:45:08,874
- Hey, watch it.
- Hey, hey! Take it easy!

569
00:45:08,957 --> 00:45:12,002
<i>A fight? Don't know how to fight.</i>

570
00:45:12,085 --> 00:45:13,545
<i>Or do I?</i>

571
00:45:22,137 --> 00:45:23,138
Ah!

572
00:45:23,221 --> 00:45:26,058
<i>You use the fishhook action
to get inside the mouth.</i>

573
00:45:26,933 --> 00:45:29,352
- <i>Like this.</i>

574
00:45:38,111 --> 00:45:41,990
<i>Attack the sensitive areas.
The eyes, the throat, the groin.</i>

575
00:45:46,661 --> 00:45:49,706
<i>If you are hit, do not stop.</i>

576
00:45:49,790 --> 00:45:52,375
<i>Your persistence will
discourage your attacker.</i>

577
00:45:54,086 --> 00:45:55,212
<i>Off-balancing your opponent</i>

578
00:45:55,295 --> 00:45:58,173
<i>and then using his momentum,
take him down.</i>

579
00:46:29,204 --> 00:46:31,540
<i>When it finally stopped,</i>

580
00:46:31,623 --> 00:46:35,168
<i>I couldn't account
for the last 18 hours of my life.</i>

581
00:46:57,274 --> 00:46:59,943
<i>Hey, it's me.
I thought you were coming over.</i>

582
00:47:00,026 --> 00:47:02,779
<i>Is everything OK?
Call me when you get this.</i>

583
00:47:42,402 --> 00:47:47,199
<i>Even off NZT, I decided
to give Van Loon's files a shot.</i>

584
00:47:54,331 --> 00:47:56,625
<i>They were hieroglyphs.</i>

585
00:48:00,003 --> 00:48:02,589
- Hello?
<i>- Kevin, this is Eddie.</i>

586
00:48:02,672 --> 00:48:03,673
- Eddie?
<i>- Listen,</i>

587
00:48:03,757 --> 00:48:06,718
<i>- I can't make the meeting today.</i>
- What are you talking about?

588
00:48:08,053 --> 00:48:09,429
I can't come in today, I'm sick.

589
00:48:09,512 --> 00:48:11,723
Yeah, well you can't
have the flu right now.

590
00:48:11,806 --> 00:48:13,099
He'll never give you this chance again.

591
00:48:13,183 --> 00:48:18,772
I know, listen, I just...
I have all this data to analyze...

592
00:48:18,855 --> 00:48:21,608
<i>What have you been doing
for the last day?</i>

593
00:48:23,068 --> 00:48:25,946
- Kev, I...
- Don't you get it? This is your test.

594
00:48:26,655 --> 00:48:29,616
Yeah, well, I can't
pass the test right now.

595
00:48:29,699 --> 00:48:31,826
And how am I gonna look if you don't?

596
00:48:31,910 --> 00:48:34,621
<i>- I know, listen, I just...</i>
- Sh. Listen to me.

597
00:48:34,704 --> 00:48:37,457
Pull yourself together.
Get down to the meeting.

598
00:48:43,964 --> 00:48:47,509
- OK, OK.
- I'll be there. I'll be there.

599
00:49:08,989 --> 00:49:11,283
- Hey, how are ya?
- Eddie.

600
00:49:11,366 --> 00:49:13,034
Good to see you again.

601
00:49:14,286 --> 00:49:15,620
Sit down.

602
00:49:19,499 --> 00:49:21,084
What do you know about Hank Atwood?

603
00:49:21,751 --> 00:49:23,003
Atwood, um...

604
00:49:24,379 --> 00:49:26,965
He's an iconoclast, um...

605
00:49:30,051 --> 00:49:31,970
Owns about half of Colorado.

606
00:49:33,471 --> 00:49:35,056
So this is prepared, Eddie?

607
00:49:35,140 --> 00:49:37,142
Uh...

608
00:49:37,225 --> 00:49:40,395
Is this Atwood 101?
Doesn't everybody know about Atwood?

609
00:49:40,478 --> 00:49:42,147
Where was he two years ago?

610
00:49:44,607 --> 00:49:47,485
- Hm?
- Nowhere.

611
00:49:49,237 --> 00:49:52,240
Two years ago he wasn't
even on <i>Forbes'</i> radar.

612
00:49:53,616 --> 00:49:56,119
That was a great leap forward, right?

613
00:49:56,995 --> 00:50:00,749
I'm baffled by this guy.
He comes out of nowhere so strong.

614
00:50:00,832 --> 00:50:03,752
Has me on the run before I can
even get to anybody in his camp.

615
00:50:03,835 --> 00:50:05,503
Beats me out of two properties,

616
00:50:05,587 --> 00:50:08,173
investing in countries with no oil.
Places I'd never go near...

617
00:50:15,555 --> 00:50:17,891
<i>We're here in front
of the hotel where a woman,</i>

618
00:50:17,974 --> 00:50:21,728
<i>identified as Maria Winberg,
was found dead in her room last night,</i>

619
00:50:21,811 --> 00:50:26,524
<i>a victim of foul play. Ms. Winberg
was a well-known socialite and model...</i>

620
00:50:27,984 --> 00:50:32,238
<i>...but was also known for her dedicated
charity work around the city.</i>

621
00:50:32,322 --> 00:50:35,492
<i>Police spent hours questioning
hotel staff and patrons</i>

622
00:50:35,575 --> 00:50:38,787
<i>and an eyewitness reported
seeing a man leaving the scene...</i>

623
00:50:38,870 --> 00:50:42,582
...help me convince him that I can
raise his game. Got any ideas?

624
00:50:42,665 --> 00:50:44,417
What do you think?

625
00:50:45,794 --> 00:50:47,629
- Hm?
- Um...

626
00:50:47,712 --> 00:50:50,173
<i>...hotel has adequate
security cameras...</i>

627
00:50:54,469 --> 00:50:57,305
You're not one of those
kind of guys, are you Eddie?

628
00:50:57,389 --> 00:50:59,474
We lose you if there's
a screen in the room?

629
00:51:01,726 --> 00:51:04,437
These are tectonic plates
that are shifting right under us.

630
00:51:04,521 --> 00:51:06,147
Pardon me, one second.

631
00:51:28,294 --> 00:51:31,881
<i>Was it possible?
Could I have killed someone?</i>

632
00:51:33,925 --> 00:51:36,052
<i>Was it even me?</i>

633
00:51:40,723 --> 00:51:43,351
<i>Eddie? This is Melissa.</i>

634
00:51:43,435 --> 00:51:45,603
<i>Listen, Vernon hasn't been
giving you anything, has he?</i>

635
00:51:45,687 --> 00:51:47,313
<i>Because that stuff is dangerous.</i>

636
00:51:47,397 --> 00:51:48,857
Melissa?

637
00:51:48,940 --> 00:51:51,276
Melissa, hey, do you think
that we can meet somewhere?

638
00:51:51,359 --> 00:51:54,028
- I really need to talk to you.
<i>- We're talking now.</i>

639
00:51:54,112 --> 00:51:57,449
No, no, let's meet at, uh...
Billy's on Grant at noon?

640
00:51:57,532 --> 00:51:59,868
<i>- You can't see me, Eddie.</i>
- No, no...

641
00:51:59,951 --> 00:52:03,037
<i>- Eddie, please.</i>
- Please, please come, please come, I...

642
00:52:03,121 --> 00:52:06,207
It's important, I really need to know
what you're talking about.

643
00:52:08,793 --> 00:52:12,046
<i>I realized that there were
other people who might know about NZT.</i>

644
00:52:12,130 --> 00:52:14,507
<i>Vernon's other clients.</i>

645
00:52:54,297 --> 00:52:55,256
<i>- Hi.</i>
- Yeah, hi,

646
00:52:55,340 --> 00:52:56,799
may I please speak with Jerry Brady?

647
00:52:56,883 --> 00:52:59,260
<i>- Jerry's in the hospital.</i>
- He's what?

648
00:52:59,344 --> 00:53:01,221
<i>Yeah, he's not doing well at all.</i>

649
00:53:01,304 --> 00:53:06,142
- Yeah, hello, is Paul Kaplan there?
<i>- Uh, no. No, he's not.</i>

650
00:53:06,226 --> 00:53:09,521
<i>Uh, Paul passed away three days ago.</i>

651
00:53:10,647 --> 00:53:12,398
Oh, my God, I'm so sorry.

652
00:53:12,482 --> 00:53:15,068
<i>Yeah, he started getting headaches,
then he collapsed at work.</i>

653
00:53:15,151 --> 00:53:16,569
<i>Now he's in the ICU.</i>

654
00:53:16,653 --> 00:53:18,863
OK, please give him my best.
Thank you.

655
00:53:18,947 --> 00:53:24,327
<i>Of all the people I called...
three were dead and the rest were sick.</i>

656
00:53:47,267 --> 00:53:51,896
Hello? Hello?

657
00:54:10,081 --> 00:54:11,082
Watch it, man!

658
00:54:28,391 --> 00:54:29,475
Did you see that?

659
00:54:31,019 --> 00:54:32,020
Hey!

660
00:54:37,859 --> 00:54:41,279
Go! Go!

661
00:55:08,139 --> 00:55:09,432
Eddie.

662
00:55:11,476 --> 00:55:13,311
Melissa.

663
00:55:20,568 --> 00:55:21,861
Hey.

664
00:55:25,782 --> 00:55:29,077
Don't tell me I look good,
'cause I know.

665
00:55:31,037 --> 00:55:33,039
I didn't want you to see me this way.

666
00:55:33,665 --> 00:55:35,500
You look good.

667
00:55:35,583 --> 00:55:38,294
Oh, I, uh...

668
00:55:38,378 --> 00:55:40,421
Yeah, I sorta got myself together.

669
00:55:40,505 --> 00:55:42,965
NZT will do that to you.

670
00:55:43,049 --> 00:55:45,968
- What do you mean?
- I mean, I took it, too.

671
00:55:46,928 --> 00:55:49,681
Vernon didn't tell you
any of this, did he?

672
00:55:49,764 --> 00:55:50,848
No.

673
00:55:50,932 --> 00:55:52,642
Well, when he told me
about this amazing new drug,

674
00:55:52,725 --> 00:55:56,688
I was like, down the hatch.
And it was. It was amazing.

675
00:55:56,771 --> 00:56:00,441
I read Brian Greene's
The Elegant Universe in 45 minutes

676
00:56:00,525 --> 00:56:02,360
and I understood it.

677
00:56:02,443 --> 00:56:04,737
My work rate increased
just insanely overnight

678
00:56:04,821 --> 00:56:06,531
and my boss started to hate me.

679
00:56:08,324 --> 00:56:13,204
They offered me his job and then...
and then I got scared.

680
00:56:14,122 --> 00:56:16,374
- Why?
- Why? Because I'm not stupid.

681
00:56:16,457 --> 00:56:19,711
Nobody can operate at that level
of mental activity and not crash.

682
00:56:22,547 --> 00:56:24,424
I stopped taking it.

683
00:56:24,507 --> 00:56:28,845
- And?
- And I got sick. Headaches.

684
00:56:28,928 --> 00:56:34,267
Throwing up.
So I went back to Vernon.

685
00:56:34,350 --> 00:56:36,853
He told me about all the people
that were dying.

686
00:56:38,146 --> 00:56:40,106
How much have you
been taking, Eddie?

687
00:56:40,189 --> 00:56:41,858
I've been taking a lot.

688
00:56:43,443 --> 00:56:47,864
Well, maybe... maybe they've
worked out the bugs. Maybe...

689
00:56:48,740 --> 00:56:50,324
Maybe this is a different batch.

690
00:56:51,743 --> 00:56:54,579
- Yeah.
- You're off it right now, aren't you?

691
00:56:54,662 --> 00:56:57,165
- Just finish your story.
- Well...

692
00:56:57,248 --> 00:57:00,084
...I didn't take any more
and I didn't die.

693
00:57:01,419 --> 00:57:04,505
But after a while, I realized I
couldn't concentrate on anything

694
00:57:04,589 --> 00:57:05,798
for longer than ten minutes.

695
00:57:05,882 --> 00:57:10,970
I, uh... I missed deadlines,
got lazy, slow, so slow.

696
00:57:11,679 --> 00:57:14,140
That was two years ago.

697
00:57:16,100 --> 00:57:18,144
And I haven't been the same since.

698
00:57:22,857 --> 00:57:26,736
Do you have some left? Good.

699
00:57:26,819 --> 00:57:30,198
You go home and you take it. You take
the dose down, but you don't just stop.

700
00:57:30,281 --> 00:57:32,033
You'll die if you just stop.

701
00:57:32,658 --> 00:57:37,455
Try to taper off, otherwise
the headaches are just the beginning.

702
00:57:39,624 --> 00:57:41,000
I have to go.

703
00:57:42,919 --> 00:57:45,463
- Do you know who makes it?
- I don't know.

704
00:57:47,048 --> 00:57:48,508
I have to go.

705
00:58:19,205 --> 00:58:24,001
Oh, shit. Listen, listen, listen.
Listen, listen, listen... OK.

706
00:58:24,085 --> 00:58:25,795
- You forget about me, huh?
- No, no, no, no.

707
00:58:25,878 --> 00:58:27,839
12:00 and you're not here.

708
00:58:27,922 --> 00:58:31,092
- I'm here now. Oh!
- Good.

709
00:58:31,175 --> 00:58:34,220
- Come on, get going.
- I could write you a check.

710
00:58:34,303 --> 00:58:39,267
Check. Check.
Are you out of your mind?

711
00:58:39,350 --> 00:58:41,602
What do you think I am?
Some financial institution?

712
00:58:41,686 --> 00:58:43,771
All right. You're right.
Look, we just need to go to a bank...

713
00:58:48,359 --> 00:58:50,736
What do you got there?

714
00:58:55,032 --> 00:58:57,451
- What is it?
- No, it's just aspirin.

715
00:58:57,535 --> 00:59:03,040
Don't look like no aspirin I ever see.
It's something good, huh?

716
00:59:21,809 --> 00:59:25,938
I feel good, man.
What's in this stuff?

717
00:59:26,022 --> 00:59:28,816
It's just, uh, vitamins and aspirin.

718
00:59:28,900 --> 00:59:32,612
You full of shit, Eddie baby.

719
00:59:58,095 --> 01:00:01,057
Lindy. I wouldn't
have let him in, but...

720
01:00:01,140 --> 01:00:03,893
I'm sorry... Tyler, I gotta go.

721
01:00:05,102 --> 01:00:06,771
I'm sick.

722
01:00:08,272 --> 01:00:12,151
I shouldn't... I'm sorry.
I couldn't make it home.

723
01:00:12,234 --> 01:00:15,321
- Let's get you to a hospital.
- No, no, no, no, no. I don't need...

724
01:00:15,404 --> 01:00:16,447
I need you to get...

725
01:00:16,530 --> 01:00:20,534
It's really simple, babe.
I need my pills.

726
01:00:20,618 --> 01:00:23,204
- Eddie, what pills?
- I don't... Babe, it's complicated.

727
01:00:23,287 --> 01:00:26,040
What are you taking?
Are you on drugs?

728
01:00:26,123 --> 01:00:29,043
They're... it's...

729
01:00:29,126 --> 01:00:32,463
<i>I had no choice.
I told Lindy everything.</i>

730
01:00:34,131 --> 01:00:38,552
So all this energy of yours,
all this focus...

731
01:00:38,636 --> 01:00:39,929
...has been a drug.

732
01:00:40,012 --> 01:00:42,390
Well, it was supposed to be legal.

733
01:00:42,473 --> 01:00:46,060
I have a... a supply stashed.

734
01:00:47,812 --> 01:00:49,563
You want me to go get it.

735
01:00:49,647 --> 01:00:53,109
- No. No, no, no. I'm gonna go get it.
- Give me your keys.

736
01:00:53,192 --> 01:00:54,860
I'll go to your apartment.

737
01:00:54,944 --> 01:00:58,823
It's not... in my apartment.
I moved it.

738
01:00:58,906 --> 01:01:02,702
OK, so where did you move it?

739
01:01:09,500 --> 01:01:11,377
In my house.

740
01:01:13,212 --> 01:01:14,672
Unbelievable.

741
01:01:26,934 --> 01:01:29,687
Hey, have you got it?

742
01:01:29,770 --> 01:01:31,355
Yeah.

743
01:01:33,399 --> 01:01:34,942
There's someone following me.

744
01:01:36,527 --> 01:01:37,737
Are you sure?

745
01:01:37,820 --> 01:01:40,281
Yeah. He got into the cab behind me

746
01:01:40,364 --> 01:01:42,366
and he's making every turn I'm making.

747
01:01:42,450 --> 01:01:46,287
- Baby, call the cops.
- Can you go around him?

748
01:01:46,370 --> 01:01:47,621
<i>Babe, what's happening?</i>

749
01:01:47,705 --> 01:01:49,498
We're stopped dead.

750
01:01:50,791 --> 01:01:53,169
He's getting out.

751
01:01:53,252 --> 01:01:55,796
Eddie, he's walking over here.

752
01:01:55,880 --> 01:01:56,881
Lindy?

753
01:01:57,798 --> 01:01:58,883
Oh, God!

754
01:02:00,718 --> 01:02:01,886
Ow!

755
01:02:13,981 --> 01:02:16,984
Help! Help!

756
01:02:18,569 --> 01:02:21,155
This man's...
This man's chasing me!

757
01:02:21,238 --> 01:02:24,784
He followed me from
my building. I don't know him.

758
01:02:24,867 --> 01:02:28,245
- Please!
- Yo, buddy, let's leave her alone!

759
01:02:30,748 --> 01:02:32,917
Are you deaf?
I said leave her alone.

760
01:02:35,002 --> 01:02:37,129
Hey, buddy, are you
trying to piss me off?

761
01:02:38,923 --> 01:02:41,175
What are you doing, man?

762
01:03:12,706 --> 01:03:15,626
- Are you still there?
- Yeah, I'm here. What happened?

763
01:03:15,709 --> 01:03:18,879
I'm stuck. He'll find me.

764
01:03:18,963 --> 01:03:20,965
<i>OK, stop talking. Don't move.</i>

765
01:03:21,048 --> 01:03:23,717
<i>- He killed two guys.
- What?</i>

766
01:03:23,801 --> 01:03:26,971
- He's gonna kill me, too.
- No, no, no...

767
01:03:27,054 --> 01:03:28,222
Can he see you?

768
01:03:31,892 --> 01:03:33,435
No.

769
01:03:34,645 --> 01:03:36,021
Not yet.

770
01:03:37,398 --> 01:03:39,942
I don't know what to do.

771
01:03:40,025 --> 01:03:43,737
OK, I need you to go into the bag
and take one of the pills.

772
01:03:44,572 --> 01:03:45,823
Why?

773
01:03:45,906 --> 01:03:48,325
<i>Because you'll know what to do.
You'll take it and then you'll know.</i>

774
01:03:48,409 --> 01:03:52,329
He's got a knife. Eddie, I can't
think my way out of a knife.

775
01:03:53,539 --> 01:03:55,624
Just listen to me.
It'll come on in 30 seconds.

776
01:03:55,708 --> 01:03:57,585
And you'll think your way out.
That's what it does.

777
01:04:21,108 --> 01:04:24,695
He's getting closer.

778
01:04:27,323 --> 01:04:28,824
Lindy, I love you.

779
01:05:00,522 --> 01:05:02,441
Hey!

780
01:05:40,145 --> 01:05:41,730
Now turn to me...

781
01:05:43,565 --> 01:05:45,275
Hey, hey, hey, hey!

782
01:05:47,069 --> 01:05:49,655
- Ah!
- Daddy!

783
01:05:49,738 --> 01:05:51,281
Ah!

784
01:05:59,999 --> 01:06:01,583
Eddie. Here.

785
01:06:02,710 --> 01:06:04,795
It's gonna be OK.

786
01:06:07,089 --> 01:06:10,592
We've got to get out of here. Eddie?

787
01:06:44,710 --> 01:06:46,086
You OK?

788
01:06:47,546 --> 01:06:49,339
Yeah.

789
01:06:51,216 --> 01:06:54,553
I'm so sorry about what happened.

790
01:06:54,636 --> 01:06:57,723
I want you to know that you never,
ever have to go through that again,

791
01:06:57,806 --> 01:07:00,559
'cause I can take care of us now.

792
01:07:00,642 --> 01:07:02,686
I'm sure you can.

793
01:07:02,770 --> 01:07:05,230
I can make us safe.
I'm back, OK?

794
01:07:09,568 --> 01:07:11,862
- Who's back, Eddie?
- Me.

795
01:07:11,945 --> 01:07:14,364
No. It's not you.

796
01:07:14,448 --> 01:07:18,035
- You were different before.
- I'm still the same person, Lin.

797
01:07:18,118 --> 01:07:19,745
I wasn't, when I was on it.

798
01:07:20,954 --> 01:07:23,082
I did things I would never do.

799
01:07:23,165 --> 01:07:25,751
- Those things saved your life.
- But they weren't me.

800
01:07:25,834 --> 01:07:27,377
Yes, they were.
No, the way it works...

801
01:07:27,461 --> 01:07:29,922
I know how it works. I get it.

802
01:07:30,005 --> 01:07:32,800
I totally get it.
You feel invincible.

803
01:07:34,051 --> 01:07:36,720
Where it will take us, Lin,
you can't even imagine.

804
01:07:36,804 --> 01:07:41,225
The things we'll be able to do.
Where we'll be a year from now.

805
01:07:41,308 --> 01:07:44,978
The kind of life that
we'll finally be able to live.

806
01:07:45,062 --> 01:07:48,273
Well, you might have
to live it without me.

807
01:07:52,319 --> 01:07:54,947
Look, I am gonna stop taking it.
There's just some things I have to do

808
01:07:55,030 --> 01:07:58,575
to keep us safe and that... To set us up
and then I'm gonna get off it.

809
01:07:58,659 --> 01:08:00,244
- I mean, that's the plan.
- Eddie...

810
01:08:00,327 --> 01:08:02,621
Look, I have a plan. I swear.

811
01:08:02,704 --> 01:08:04,498
I'm gonna get off it.

812
01:08:05,958 --> 01:08:07,292
OK.

813
01:08:09,253 --> 01:08:10,838
I hope you do.

814
01:08:43,036 --> 01:08:44,913
Looking for me?

815
01:08:47,291 --> 01:08:50,961
You think you can run out on me, huh?
You think I don't know where you are?

816
01:08:51,044 --> 01:08:52,796
I was under the impression
our business was settled.

817
01:08:52,880 --> 01:08:54,673
I want some more of that stuff.

818
01:08:54,756 --> 01:08:57,718
Or I shoot you right here.
Take your pick.

819
01:08:57,801 --> 01:08:59,595
<i>This guy was not going away.</i>

820
01:08:59,678 --> 01:09:01,430
<i>A few pills would buy me some time.</i>

821
01:09:01,513 --> 01:09:04,892
- There it is. We're done.
- Oh, yeah. We'll see.

822
01:09:04,975 --> 01:09:07,519
<i>Until I found a more...
permanent solution.</i>

823
01:09:08,353 --> 01:09:12,107
Unless you see a real threat, I never
want it known that I have any security.

824
01:09:12,191 --> 01:09:14,401
You'll never precede me,
you'll always follow me.

825
01:09:14,484 --> 01:09:17,946
Never more than 15 steps behind.
And never stand too close together.

826
01:09:18,030 --> 01:09:20,741
I want to able to look behind me
if the opportunity presents itself.

827
01:09:22,159 --> 01:09:24,411
And don't wear the same color suit.
This isn't The Matrix.

828
01:09:30,167 --> 01:09:32,336
You have to know, Eddie,
I wrote you off.

829
01:09:32,419 --> 01:09:34,046
- I was sick.
- You don't get sick.

830
01:09:34,129 --> 01:09:36,840
You're playing at this level, you get
hit by a car, you don't even die.

831
01:09:36,924 --> 01:09:39,593
- You're absolutely right.
- I know I'm right.

832
01:09:39,676 --> 01:09:41,553
I mean, I didn't know who or what
I was talking to.

833
01:09:41,637 --> 01:09:44,848
A hundred and five. That's who
you were talking to, Carl. Delirium.

834
01:09:44,932 --> 01:09:47,476
Mmm-hm.

835
01:09:47,559 --> 01:09:49,269
I sent over my revised projections.

836
01:09:49,353 --> 01:09:50,270
I didn't ask for your projections.

837
01:09:50,896 --> 01:09:53,440
I know, but I think if
you take a look at them...

838
01:09:53,523 --> 01:09:55,525
I already have.
As a matter of fact...

839
01:09:57,069 --> 01:10:00,280
...I fired a few people
over your projections.

840
01:10:00,364 --> 01:10:01,990
- I'm sorry.
- No, you're not.

841
01:10:02,074 --> 01:10:04,868
You're relieved that you know things
my team missed.

842
01:10:04,952 --> 01:10:09,498
The minute,
the instant that's no longer true...

843
01:10:12,417 --> 01:10:14,002
<i>Two months after I'd started NZT,</i>

844
01:10:14,086 --> 01:10:17,172
<i>I was brokering the biggest merger
in corporate history.</i>

845
01:10:17,256 --> 01:10:21,677
Ironically, it's gonna be crude. Crude
is gonna be where we're gonna hit them.

846
01:10:21,760 --> 01:10:24,388
Which yields a prospective profit
of 200 percent.

847
01:10:24,471 --> 01:10:26,348
That makes both of us
very happy campers.

848
01:10:26,431 --> 01:10:28,809
<i>I found that if
I maintained an even dose,</i>

849
01:10:28,892 --> 01:10:31,228
<i>remembered to eat,
drank no alcohol,</i>

850
01:10:31,311 --> 01:10:33,730
<i>the blackouts didn't recur.</i>
- How you doing, Eddie?

851
01:10:34,690 --> 01:10:37,651
Tom, is it possible to construct
an imperceptible compartment?

852
01:10:37,734 --> 01:10:39,194
In this jacket?

853
01:10:39,278 --> 01:10:42,698
- In all of them.
- Certainly, sir.

854
01:10:42,781 --> 01:10:46,868
<i>Safer though my stash now was, I
was not sitting around until it ran out.</i>

855
01:10:46,952 --> 01:10:50,289
Can I combine these ingredients
in the same exact quantity, yes, but,

856
01:10:50,372 --> 01:10:55,168
the exact method of delivery
to the brain... Darts at a dartboard.

857
01:10:55,252 --> 01:10:57,879
- Meaning?
- Clinical trials, guinea pig people.

858
01:10:57,963 --> 01:11:00,465
- That's gonna take too long.
- It's what it is.

859
01:11:00,549 --> 01:11:04,052
Or you'll kill people.
You need 12, to 18 months...

860
01:11:04,136 --> 01:11:06,263
I'll give you two million dollars
if you can do it in six.

861
01:11:14,813 --> 01:11:18,734
I found that we can cut costs
30 percent and, um...

862
01:11:22,154 --> 01:11:23,488
Excuse me one second.

863
01:11:26,992 --> 01:11:29,202
I thought we straightened this out,
Detective.

864
01:11:29,286 --> 01:11:33,206
This isn't about Vernon Gant,
Mr. Morra.

865
01:11:33,290 --> 01:11:35,667
What is this about?

866
01:11:35,751 --> 01:11:41,089
The witness identified the Maria Winberg
suspect as this person.

867
01:11:45,761 --> 01:11:48,680
Wanna tell me your whereabouts
the night of April 2nd?

868
01:11:50,640 --> 01:11:52,851
<i>I couldn't keep
this quiet on my own.</i>

869
01:11:54,019 --> 01:11:58,523
<i>Enter Morris Brandt...
the most lethal lawyer in New York.</i>

870
01:12:01,526 --> 01:12:06,198
You're lucky somebody wiped the room.
It's weak. Circumstantial at best.

871
01:12:06,281 --> 01:12:09,284
Just between us, were you there?

872
01:12:09,368 --> 01:12:10,619
I don't remember.

873
01:12:12,245 --> 01:12:13,747
I don't want to
make concessions to you...

874
01:12:13,830 --> 01:12:15,874
<i>Carl Van Loon and Hank Atwood</i>

875
01:12:15,957 --> 01:12:19,961
<i>had no idea I might
soon be charged with murder.</i>

876
01:12:20,045 --> 01:12:22,714
But SyCorps, Andine
and others are jockeying

877
01:12:22,798 --> 01:12:24,800
for the same industrial
concessions in Libya

878
01:12:24,883 --> 01:12:27,135
that both our companies are
pretending we're not sniffing out.

879
01:12:27,219 --> 01:12:28,595
How do you know this?

880
01:12:29,596 --> 01:12:32,265
The governmental bribe system
is itself corrupt, so, of course,

881
01:12:32,349 --> 01:12:35,477
information about its inner workings,
like anything, can be bought.

882
01:12:38,063 --> 01:12:39,439
Go on.

883
01:12:39,523 --> 01:12:42,901
If you take a look at the lower
left-hand column, in 2008,

884
01:12:42,984 --> 01:12:46,405
five companies were jockeying for a
position before we even took notice.

885
01:12:49,783 --> 01:12:51,493
Jesus, he looks frail.

886
01:12:52,536 --> 01:12:54,913
- Could be an act.
- He's not even 60.

887
01:12:57,249 --> 01:13:00,293
So, have you decided, Eddie?
What are you gonna do?

888
01:13:00,377 --> 01:13:03,463
When this is over? I don't know.

889
01:13:03,547 --> 01:13:04,923
You haven't given me the answer.

890
01:13:05,006 --> 01:13:06,842
You haven't asked the question.

891
01:13:06,925 --> 01:13:08,677
Well, given the scale
of my contribution,

892
01:13:08,760 --> 01:13:11,096
let's say 45.

893
01:13:11,179 --> 01:13:14,182
Done. Forty-five thousand dollars.

894
01:13:14,266 --> 01:13:17,686
Forty million is plenty, Eddie.
There's a lot more where that came from.

895
01:13:17,769 --> 01:13:20,063
You know, Eddie...

896
01:13:20,147 --> 01:13:21,189
...it may be on your mind

897
01:13:21,273 --> 01:13:23,692
that you're not gonna
continue to work for me any more.

898
01:13:23,775 --> 01:13:25,485
This has been
the learning experience of my life.

899
01:13:25,569 --> 01:13:28,029
I hope it has. I hope you don't
think you've gotten enough from me.

900
01:13:28,113 --> 01:13:29,656
On to the next.

901
01:13:29,739 --> 01:13:32,492
Well, in order for a career to evolve,
I'm gonna have to move on.

902
01:13:32,576 --> 01:13:35,036
That you would even think that
would only show me

903
01:13:35,120 --> 01:13:37,539
how unprepared you are
to be on your own.

904
01:13:37,622 --> 01:13:40,000
- I mean, you do know you're a freak.

905
01:13:40,083 --> 01:13:42,586
Your deductive powers are a gift
from God, or chance,

906
01:13:42,669 --> 01:13:45,005
or a stray shot of sperm,
or whatever, or whoever the hell wrote

907
01:13:45,088 --> 01:13:47,382
your life script, a gift not earned.

908
01:13:47,466 --> 01:13:51,344
You do not know what I know because
you have not earned those powers.

909
01:13:51,428 --> 01:13:53,138
You're careless with those powers.

910
01:13:53,221 --> 01:13:56,516
You flaunt them and you throw them
around like a brat with his trust fund.

911
01:13:56,600 --> 01:13:59,561
You haven't had to climb up
all the greasy little rungs.

912
01:13:59,644 --> 01:14:02,606
You haven't been bored blind at the
fundraisers. You haven't done the time

913
01:14:02,689 --> 01:14:04,524
in that first marriage
to the girl with the right father.

914
01:14:04,608 --> 01:14:07,319
You think you can leap
over all in a single bound.

915
01:14:07,402 --> 01:14:09,905
You haven't had to bribe
or charm or threaten your way

916
01:14:09,988 --> 01:14:13,200
to a seat at that table. You don't
know how to assess your competition

917
01:14:13,283 --> 01:14:16,495
because you haven't competed.
Don't make me your competition.

918
01:14:19,331 --> 01:14:21,374
Now, I'll open up
a line of credit for you.

919
01:14:21,458 --> 01:14:23,502
You'll be wanting a few toys.

920
01:14:41,186 --> 01:14:43,855
<i>Hotel rooms
were no longer an option.</i>

921
01:14:43,939 --> 01:14:45,941
<i>What I needed was a bunker.</i>

922
01:14:46,024 --> 01:14:48,443
<i>There's a built-in safe,
central surveillance,</i>

923
01:14:48,527 --> 01:14:51,112
<i>vault grade pneumatic doors.</i>

924
01:14:51,196 --> 01:14:54,491
It's an unparalleled
three-tier security system.

925
01:14:54,574 --> 01:14:57,953
A fortress with a hell of a view.

926
01:15:02,207 --> 01:15:04,960
- What's the asking price?
- Eight-point-five.

927
01:15:09,965 --> 01:15:11,341
I'll take it.

928
01:15:16,972 --> 01:15:19,808
<i>Right away, it was obvious
he was on NZT.</i>

929
01:15:19,891 --> 01:15:22,561
Next week, I'm gonna need 20 pills.

930
01:15:22,644 --> 01:15:24,604
- Next week, you can go fuck yourself!

931
01:15:24,688 --> 01:15:26,273
Not that you'd feel anything.

932
01:15:27,232 --> 01:15:30,902
I don't think your Fortune 500
financial friends would appreciate

933
01:15:30,986 --> 01:15:34,114
the details of your little dilemma
with the police.

934
01:15:35,073 --> 01:15:37,826
Thursday, I'm gonna need the pills here.

935
01:15:40,870 --> 01:15:42,289
Getting smarter!

936
01:15:44,165 --> 01:15:46,167
<i>Obviously, this could not go on,</i>

937
01:15:46,251 --> 01:15:49,421
<i>but there is very little on this earth
that 40 million dollars can't solve.</i>

938
01:15:49,504 --> 01:15:53,133
<i>And tomorrow at nine,
Atwood would sign the papers.</i>

939
01:16:00,473 --> 01:16:03,476
- Do you want to call it?
- Cold feet?

940
01:16:03,560 --> 01:16:06,146
There was all last night
to tell us that.

941
01:16:07,689 --> 01:16:10,942
- Anything?
<i>- I'll call him again, sir.</i>

942
01:16:11,026 --> 01:16:13,236
OK, the decision could
have changed. That makes sense.

943
01:16:13,320 --> 01:16:16,698
The discourtesy doesn't.
It's still on.

944
01:16:17,490 --> 01:16:19,701
Oh, based on your charts
and projections.

945
01:16:20,660 --> 01:16:22,871
You know, remind me,
who the fuck are you, anyway?

946
01:16:22,954 --> 01:16:25,665
I'm sorry, Carl, I've had it with this
unqualified, posturing little prick.

947
01:16:25,749 --> 01:16:27,876
- Pull it back.
- Since when is this the Delphic Oracle?

948
01:16:27,959 --> 01:16:31,796
- Pull it back.
- Excuse me, Mr. Van Loon?

949
01:16:31,880 --> 01:16:33,340
Mrs. Atwood is in your office.

950
01:16:36,259 --> 01:16:38,678
Ah. Mrs. Atwood, Carl Van Loon.

951
01:16:38,762 --> 01:16:41,181
- My associate, Edward Morra.
- Pleasure.

952
01:16:43,641 --> 01:16:46,227
I wanted to make this
as confidential as possible.

953
01:16:46,311 --> 01:16:47,896
Of course.

954
01:16:47,979 --> 01:16:51,900
My husband experienced some pain
and dizziness this morning.

955
01:16:51,983 --> 01:16:54,527
He's currently at Lenox Hill,
undergoing tests.

956
01:16:55,737 --> 01:16:57,697
I just want you to know
we have every intention

957
01:16:57,781 --> 01:16:59,491
of signing the contract.

958
01:17:00,325 --> 01:17:03,536
And as soon as he is able,
we will proceed.

959
01:17:04,704 --> 01:17:07,624
I realize this is a useless platitude,
but if there's anything that we can do,

960
01:17:07,707 --> 01:17:09,042
please don't hesitate to tell us.

961
01:17:09,125 --> 01:17:10,960
Obviously, we want to be
as helpful and respectful

962
01:17:11,044 --> 01:17:12,587
as possible, Mrs. Atwood.

963
01:17:12,670 --> 01:17:15,465
Thank you, gentlemen.
I rely on your discretion.

964
01:17:17,509 --> 01:17:18,551
There won't be a proxy.

965
01:17:18,635 --> 01:17:21,679
No, a cagey prick like him
giving away power of attorney?

966
01:17:26,142 --> 01:17:28,144
Well, he'd better get better.

967
01:17:31,272 --> 01:17:35,693
<i>But Atwood wouldn't get better.
Because Atwood was out of NZT.</i>

968
01:17:39,197 --> 01:17:42,742
Well, why be surprised?

969
01:17:42,826 --> 01:17:46,246
<i>How many other meteoric rises
might be explained by NZT?</i>

970
01:17:46,329 --> 01:17:47,747
<i>At /east I had some.</i>

971
01:17:47,831 --> 01:17:49,958
<i>- My life wasn't in jeopardy.
- You can wait here.</i>

972
01:17:50,041 --> 01:17:53,044
<i>Only my money. And my liberty.</i>

973
01:17:54,462 --> 01:17:55,839
I've been all over with them.

974
01:17:55,922 --> 01:17:58,299
We can't allow
any disparity in race

975
01:17:58,383 --> 01:18:01,136
or physical type between you
and the rest of the line up.

976
01:18:01,219 --> 01:18:02,178
OK.

977
01:18:02,262 --> 01:18:04,639
They're as close to your clones
as I could possibly get.

978
01:18:04,722 --> 01:18:08,476
<i>It's gotta be just one big
handsome blur to this guy.</i>

979
01:18:22,240 --> 01:18:24,159
<i>Who was out there?</i>

980
01:18:25,910 --> 01:18:27,787
<i>The woman's husband?</i>

981
01:18:28,580 --> 01:18:30,498
<i>A hotel maid?</i>

982
01:18:32,459 --> 01:18:34,961
<i>Was this how it was all going to end?</i>

983
01:18:39,299 --> 01:18:41,843
Not the dimmest clue.
He was dithering.

984
01:18:41,926 --> 01:18:44,846
"He was the third one from the right.
No. Not him."

985
01:18:44,929 --> 01:18:50,351
There you go. It's a thing of beauty.
You have that made?

986
01:18:50,435 --> 01:18:52,020
I did.

987
01:19:08,870 --> 01:19:10,705
<i>...market's been mixed today.</i>

988
01:19:10,788 --> 01:19:13,500
<i>The Dow has been yo-yoing
amidst speculation that.</i>

989
01:19:13,583 --> 01:19:18,296
<i>Van Loon Associates and Hank Atwood
have negotiated a merger...</i>

990
01:19:19,422 --> 01:19:21,174
Have you been talking to anyone?

991
01:19:22,592 --> 01:19:25,386
No, I haven't been talking to anybody,
Carl. I'm not stupid.

992
01:19:25,470 --> 01:19:26,721
I know you're not stupid, Eddie,

993
01:19:26,804 --> 01:19:28,723
but don't make the classic
smart person's mistake.

994
01:19:28,806 --> 01:19:30,683
Thinking no one's smarter than you.

995
01:19:30,767 --> 01:19:33,895
Where the hell have you been? This is
the second time you've turned to vapor.

996
01:19:33,978 --> 01:19:36,856
I was in the building, I was getting
coffee. Why, has there been movement?

997
01:19:36,940 --> 01:19:39,234
Yeah, there's been movement alright.
Atwood's in a coma.

998
01:19:39,317 --> 01:19:41,861
He wasn't playing us.
He's dying.

999
01:19:41,945 --> 01:19:43,530
What do you know about this?

1000
01:19:43,613 --> 01:19:47,200
<i>I was less than
usually equipped to answer.</i>

1001
01:19:47,283 --> 01:19:51,621
<i>The dullness was coming on.
I was overdue to take my NZT.</i>

1002
01:19:51,704 --> 01:19:54,916
<i>And the pain was
starting behind my eyes.</i>

1003
01:19:54,999 --> 01:19:56,251
Excuse me.

1004
01:19:56,334 --> 01:19:57,835
You're not excused.

1005
01:19:57,919 --> 01:20:00,463
What? A man in your employ
can't take a piss, Carl?

1006
01:20:23,152 --> 01:20:25,029
<i>Was it Brandt or someone else?</i>

1007
01:20:25,113 --> 01:20:27,699
<i>The police station?
A coatroom?</i>

1008
01:20:41,713 --> 01:20:42,964
Mr. Morra.

1009
01:20:43,047 --> 01:20:45,550
- Carl, I'm sorry...
- Mr. Morra. Mr. Morra!

1010
01:20:45,633 --> 01:20:47,385
- This just came for you.
- Yeah, what?

1011
01:20:51,973 --> 01:20:55,893
Hope not, so let me know
when you know.

1012
01:20:56,603 --> 01:20:58,563
All right, I'm gonna ask you again,
Eddie. What do you know about this?

1013
01:20:58,646 --> 01:21:01,608
- What? About the leak?
- No. About Atwood.

1014
01:21:01,691 --> 01:21:03,526
What? What about Atwood?

1015
01:21:03,610 --> 01:21:05,862
Look, you got a long way to go
as a liar, kiddo.

1016
01:21:05,945 --> 01:21:07,155
- Carl, I don't know what you're...
- Listen!

1017
01:21:07,238 --> 01:21:08,615
What you're about to say is bullshit.

1018
01:21:08,698 --> 01:21:10,199
I know there's something
you're hiding from me.

1019
01:21:10,283 --> 01:21:12,160
I smell it on you. Right now,

1020
01:21:12,243 --> 01:21:15,622
I wanna know why this clusterfuck
of a deal is dead in the water!

1021
01:21:17,290 --> 01:21:19,334
Carl.

1022
01:21:19,417 --> 01:21:21,419
Eddie! Eddie!

1023
01:21:38,019 --> 01:21:39,896
<i>Outside Lenox Hill Hospital today,</i>

1024
01:21:39,979 --> 01:21:42,732
<i>more twists and turns
in the Van Loon-Atwood merger.</i>

1025
01:21:42,815 --> 01:21:46,611
<i>There is absolutely no truth
to the rumor of this merger.</i>

1026
01:21:46,694 --> 01:21:50,406
<i>None whatsoever.
My husband is having tests.</i>

1027
01:21:50,490 --> 01:21:52,575
<i>This is a difficult time.</i>

1028
01:21:52,658 --> 01:21:56,579
<i>We would appreciate your directing
further questions to our attorney.</i>

1029
01:21:56,662 --> 01:21:59,082
<i>Yes, we have no further comments
at this time.</i>

1030
01:21:59,165 --> 01:22:01,584
<i>We're just awaiting the results
of the latest round of tests</i>

1031
01:22:01,667 --> 01:22:02,627
<i>and hoping for the best.</i>

1032
01:22:02,710 --> 01:22:04,128
<i>Thank you.</i>

1033
01:22:43,042 --> 01:22:47,547
Eddie.
I know you're in there, Eddie.

1034
01:22:48,840 --> 01:22:51,717
Open up.

1035
01:22:53,970 --> 01:22:56,055
Come on.

1036
01:22:58,307 --> 01:23:00,184
Open the door.

1037
01:23:16,909 --> 01:23:19,620
<i>And so, here I am.</i>

1038
01:23:22,415 --> 01:23:25,084
<i>I knew they would kill me
slowly and horribly.</i>

1039
01:23:25,168 --> 01:23:28,296
<i>But this choice
would at /east be mine.</i>

1040
01:23:37,722 --> 01:23:42,059
<i>But we're instinctive creatures.
We want to live.</i>

1041
01:23:42,143 --> 01:23:46,647
<i>So my foggy brain tried to remember
where one tablet of NZT might be.</i>

1042
01:23:53,946 --> 01:23:58,701
<i>It was possible.
And possible was enough.</i>

1043
01:24:22,058 --> 01:24:23,643
Hey, Eddie.

1044
01:24:46,457 --> 01:24:49,418
Now you know I want more,
so why would you upset me?

1045
01:24:50,753 --> 01:24:53,339
I don't like being down
to this last one.

1046
01:24:53,422 --> 01:24:55,341
You don't even know how to use it.

1047
01:24:55,424 --> 01:24:57,760
You don't appreciate it.

1048
01:24:57,843 --> 01:25:00,930
You see, I dissolve in a solution.

1049
01:25:01,013 --> 01:25:05,685
You shoot it. It goes straight
into the blood and brain.

1050
01:25:07,311 --> 01:25:09,480
Lasts much longer.

1051
01:25:24,328 --> 01:25:25,913
So...

1052
01:25:25,997 --> 01:25:28,624
You gonna tell me where the rest is?

1053
01:25:28,708 --> 01:25:30,376
I'm plumb out.

1054
01:25:35,006 --> 01:25:37,717
You know, these guys are gonna find it.

1055
01:25:38,884 --> 01:25:41,429
Or I'm gonna make you tell me.

1056
01:25:41,512 --> 01:25:44,473
Question is, which is gonna come first?

1057
01:25:45,433 --> 01:25:47,560
I guess we're gonna find out, huh?

1058
01:25:51,230 --> 01:25:54,525
This is a waste of my time,
you know?

1059
01:25:54,609 --> 01:25:57,528
I'm getting out
of this side of the business.

1060
01:25:57,612 --> 01:26:02,325
I'm branching out.
I'm starting import-export company.

1061
01:26:03,200 --> 01:26:04,327
Very exciting stuff.

1062
01:26:04,410 --> 01:26:08,039
I got a lot on my plate.
You're lucky I'm here.

1063
01:26:15,963 --> 01:26:21,552
Back in the old times, I might
peel your skin back. Fillet you.

1064
01:26:21,636 --> 01:26:24,388
"Fillet." It's a nice word.

1065
01:26:24,472 --> 01:26:29,185
I didn't know this before.
Oh, I had such a poor vocabulary.

1066
01:26:34,190 --> 01:26:36,442
But then I discovered
this thing, Google.

1067
01:26:36,525 --> 01:26:40,571
Did you know the small and large
intestines is 20 foot long?

1068
01:26:40,655 --> 01:26:42,490
I had no idea.
I don't believe it.

1069
01:26:42,573 --> 01:26:46,369
I gotta see this for myself.
I'm dubious. It's another nice word.

1070
01:26:46,452 --> 01:26:50,790
Anyway, I'm sure you're curious, too.

1071
01:26:50,873 --> 01:26:53,376
Don't worry, everything's sterilized.

1072
01:26:53,459 --> 01:26:56,504
We're gonna keep you alive
for the whole shebang.

1073
01:26:56,587 --> 01:26:58,756
You're gonna get
a front row seat, Eddie.

1074
01:27:01,217 --> 01:27:03,094
What?

1075
01:27:05,429 --> 01:27:07,682
- Ah!

1076
01:27:36,168 --> 01:27:38,337
<i>Not much of a triumph, was it?</i>

1077
01:27:38,421 --> 01:27:40,631
<i>Because I would die here, too.</i>

1078
01:27:42,758 --> 01:27:47,054
<i>Only NZT could help me and the last
of it was in this fuck's bloodstream.</i>

1079
01:29:02,755 --> 01:29:04,006
Victor!

1080
01:29:11,764 --> 01:29:13,390
What?

1081
01:29:21,023 --> 01:29:21,941
Ah!

1082
01:29:27,488 --> 01:29:28,697
Hey!

1083
01:29:30,699 --> 01:29:31,450
Ah!

1084
01:29:40,501 --> 01:29:42,336
Ah!

1085
01:30:22,793 --> 01:30:23,794
Victor?

1086
01:30:27,089 --> 01:30:28,299
Victor?

1087
01:30:29,466 --> 01:30:30,801
Victor?

1088
01:31:20,434 --> 01:31:21,936
<i>The police would later note</i>

1089
01:31:22,019 --> 01:31:24,647
<i>that my apartment's previous
owner was an arms dealer.</i>

1090
01:31:24,730 --> 01:31:29,902
<i>Of course, these intruders had
been his dissatisfied clients.</i>

1091
01:31:29,985 --> 01:31:32,613
<i>And the night was still young.</i>

1092
01:31:45,084 --> 01:31:47,211
I'm sure you're curious
about what happened to your boss.

1093
01:31:47,294 --> 01:31:49,421
I know I certainly am.

1094
01:31:49,505 --> 01:31:52,341
I mean, if my attorney was
really working for Mr. Atwood,

1095
01:31:52,424 --> 01:31:56,679
trying to save his life,
why is he dead?

1096
01:31:56,762 --> 01:31:59,348
I mean, at what point was
the pony express supposed to ride in

1097
01:31:59,431 --> 01:32:01,517
with his life-saving medication?

1098
01:32:02,559 --> 01:32:04,603
<i>His boss was dead.</i>

1099
01:32:04,687 --> 01:32:08,774
<i>So, I didn't matter now.</i>

1100
01:32:08,857 --> 01:32:13,570
<i>But he did not appreciate that Morris
Brandt had kept the pills for himself.</i>

1101
01:32:25,541 --> 01:32:27,876
<i>And it was all still possible.</i>

1102
01:32:46,353 --> 01:32:48,856
Actually, no, there's no more tickets.

1103
01:32:48,939 --> 01:32:53,402
Yeah, I know. An overbooked fundraiser.
That's a first.

1104
01:32:53,485 --> 01:32:55,154
Well, here he comes. I'll ask him.

1105
01:32:55,904 --> 01:32:57,322
Oh, that's much better.

1106
01:32:57,406 --> 01:32:59,950
- Great job.
- Thank you, sir.

1107
01:33:00,034 --> 01:33:01,160
Hi, Mr. Morra.

1108
01:33:01,243 --> 01:33:03,078
Edward!

1109
01:33:04,455 --> 01:33:06,707
I think we're looking
at a second overflow event.

1110
01:33:06,790 --> 01:33:08,792
There's a wave of donations coming in
and I don't think

1111
01:33:08,876 --> 01:33:11,170
they're even gonna
try a second debate.

1112
01:33:11,253 --> 01:33:12,504
- Great.
- Hold on.

1113
01:33:12,588 --> 01:33:14,214
A guy from
Eiben-Chemcorp's in your office.

1114
01:33:14,298 --> 01:33:16,050
- I have a lunch.
- They're your biggest contributor.

1115
01:33:16,133 --> 01:33:17,342
Give him his two crappy little minutes.

1116
01:33:17,426 --> 01:33:19,803
- Eiben-Chemcorp Pharmaceuticals?
- Yeah.

1117
01:33:21,680 --> 01:33:24,391
Finally get to meet the guys
that keep the lights on.

1118
01:33:25,893 --> 01:33:27,686
- Carl!
- Mr. Morra.

1119
01:33:29,146 --> 01:33:32,649
What a surprise. They told me somebody
from Eiben was coming.

1120
01:33:32,733 --> 01:33:36,070
Chemcorp. Yeah, that's me now. I'm
moving out of the energy business.

1121
01:33:36,153 --> 01:33:39,865
Come on. That's like Hef
moving out of the mansion.

1122
01:33:39,948 --> 01:33:43,202
Yeah. I bought Eiben a couple of months
ago. I'm surprised you didn't hear.

1123
01:33:43,285 --> 01:33:47,331
No. No. Well, they really
keep me hopping here.

1124
01:33:47,414 --> 01:33:50,209
Well, it's gonna pay off,
Senator Morra.

1125
01:33:51,543 --> 01:33:53,921
The Senate. Who knows...

1126
01:33:54,004 --> 01:33:57,341
Everything's a technicality
from here on in, isn't it, Eddie?

1127
01:33:58,008 --> 01:34:02,596
I think we both know where it's headed,
if you can maintain.

1128
01:34:05,432 --> 01:34:08,644
Have you asked yourself what
you're gonna do when you run out?

1129
01:34:24,326 --> 01:34:27,329
- When did you know?
- Does it matter? I know now.

1130
01:34:28,413 --> 01:34:31,834
I wish you'd come to me at first, Eddie.
I could have bought Eiben at 33 a share.

1131
01:34:31,917 --> 01:34:33,335
We could have been partners.

1132
01:34:37,089 --> 01:34:38,924
So, Eiben makes NZT.

1133
01:34:39,007 --> 01:34:41,802
Well, it'll never officially exist.

1134
01:34:41,885 --> 01:34:44,429
Obviously, I don't need
to point out the advantages for you.

1135
01:34:45,347 --> 01:34:46,849
Oh, I think maybe you do.

1136
01:34:46,932 --> 01:34:50,853
Limitless supply.
We know you can't get any more.

1137
01:34:50,936 --> 01:34:52,437
You sure about that?

1138
01:34:52,521 --> 01:34:54,356
They shut down your lab this morning.

1139
01:34:56,859 --> 01:35:00,404
Eddie. I just want to help you,
that's all.

1140
01:35:00,487 --> 01:35:03,448
Keep you supplied.
No disruption in service.

1141
01:35:03,532 --> 01:35:05,617
Give you peace of mind.
That's all.

1142
01:35:07,286 --> 01:35:10,289
And in return, I do what?

1143
01:35:10,372 --> 01:35:14,168
Get a little advice, maybe.
I won't be bothering you much at first.

1144
01:35:14,251 --> 01:35:16,712
And later. What are you
gonna be bothering me with?

1145
01:35:16,795 --> 01:35:20,465
Well, given where you're heading,
some of our ideas are a bit ambitious.

1146
01:35:20,549 --> 01:35:22,426
But hey, I think they're achievable.

1147
01:35:22,509 --> 01:35:24,261
And what if I don't like your ideas?

1148
01:35:24,344 --> 01:35:26,722
Then we'll say, "Godspeed."

1149
01:35:26,805 --> 01:35:30,309
And your candle will have shed a brief,
but lovely light.

1150
01:35:32,186 --> 01:35:33,645
But you know what, Eddie,
let's not even

1151
01:35:33,729 --> 01:35:34,730
go there right now.

1152
01:35:34,813 --> 01:35:38,108
Right now, I just want
to keep you brilliant...

1153
01:35:38,192 --> 01:35:41,904
...and healthy. Come on.
Let's go get some lunch.

1154
01:36:00,172 --> 01:36:02,132
After you, Senator.

1155
01:36:03,675 --> 01:36:05,594
Thanks for stopping by, Carl.

1156
01:36:06,470 --> 01:36:08,222
Give us a second.

1157
01:36:08,305 --> 01:36:11,016
What are you doing? Do you want
to be President of the United States

1158
01:36:11,099 --> 01:36:13,769
- or brain dead, stuck full of tubes?
- I'll take my chances.

1159
01:36:13,852 --> 01:36:17,064
I told you your chances don't exist.
We shut down your lab.

1160
01:36:17,147 --> 01:36:18,357
You don't think some smartass on NZT

1161
01:36:18,440 --> 01:36:20,025
might have two or three
or even four labs?

1162
01:36:20,108 --> 01:36:22,277
- So what? You're not making any more.
- That's right, I'm not.

1163
01:36:22,361 --> 01:36:24,404
I thought better of it.
See, once you know what's in it,

1164
01:36:24,488 --> 01:36:28,116
you can tweak it, re-engineer it.
Get the bugs out. Taper off.

1165
01:36:28,825 --> 01:36:30,160
I'm off it, Carl.

1166
01:36:30,244 --> 01:36:31,495
Well, on it or off it,

1167
01:36:31,578 --> 01:36:33,872
there's no scenario
where you're not working for me.

1168
01:36:33,956 --> 01:36:35,457
How you gonna pay me?
In cigarettes?

1169
01:36:35,540 --> 01:36:36,708
Last year you traded

1170
01:36:36,792 --> 01:36:38,710
- inside information 17 times.
- You threatening me?

1171
01:36:38,794 --> 01:36:41,880
Blank checks from Dubai.
Dumping in Chile. Disappeared officials.

1172
01:36:41,964 --> 01:36:44,049
- I mean, you shed a spot light...
- Do you want to go to war with me?

1173
01:36:44,132 --> 01:36:46,218
And trust me when I say this,
I'm not even being creative.

1174
01:36:46,301 --> 01:36:48,262
There's no way you can be making
accusations like that

1175
01:36:48,345 --> 01:36:50,681
and be walking around alive.

1176
01:36:50,764 --> 01:36:53,350
That you would even think that shows me
how unprepared you are to fight me.

1177
01:36:53,433 --> 01:36:54,977
- You're no longer equipped to fight me.
- I may be off it,

1178
01:36:55,060 --> 01:36:57,437
but you think I didn't learn anything?
That my synapses didn't change?

1179
01:36:57,521 --> 01:36:58,855
That I kept absolutely nothing?

1180
01:36:58,939 --> 01:37:00,524
You have no idea what
kind of enemy I can be.

1181
01:37:00,607 --> 01:37:03,443
- That van's gonna rear-end that taxi.
- What?

1182
01:37:03,527 --> 01:37:06,196
The driver's distracted. He's texting.
He's going 30 miles an hour.

1183
01:37:06,280 --> 01:37:08,073
He has 60 feet to stop.
He doesn't have room.

1184
01:37:08,156 --> 01:37:09,908
- What van?

1185
01:37:13,245 --> 01:37:14,621
I see everything, Carl.

1186
01:37:14,705 --> 01:37:16,999
I'm 50 moves ahead of you
and everybody else.

1187
01:37:17,082 --> 01:37:19,418
What makes you think I don't have a guy
with a bead on you right now?

1188
01:37:19,501 --> 01:37:21,211
How do you know you're gonna be alive
this time next year?

1189
01:37:21,295 --> 01:37:24,423
What are you doing? You get
your hands off me. What are you doing?

1190
01:37:24,506 --> 01:37:27,259
Something's pumping half-mass in there.
The walls of your heart are dilated.

1191
01:37:27,342 --> 01:37:30,554
Your aortic valve's steno. So you're
gonna need to get that replaced.

1192
01:37:32,347 --> 01:37:33,974
But you already knew that.

1193
01:37:39,771 --> 01:37:41,606
You know, you should
really be glad about this.

1194
01:37:41,690 --> 01:37:44,735
'Cause me working for you?
You'd end up as my bitch.

1195
01:37:56,705 --> 01:37:59,458
Maybe we should cool off.
Talk again.

1196
01:38:01,293 --> 01:38:03,170
I don't think so.

1197
01:38:04,755 --> 01:38:06,006
Don't forget to vote.

1198
01:38:14,306 --> 01:38:15,724
Edward.

1199
01:38:15,807 --> 01:38:17,434
You good?

1200
01:38:17,517 --> 01:38:20,479
Yeah. Told you I have a lunch.

1201
01:38:26,401 --> 01:38:28,779
- Sorry I'm late.
- Hey. Don't worry.

1202
01:38:30,864 --> 01:38:33,408
- How's your day going?
- Ah. I've had better.

1203
01:38:35,243 --> 01:38:37,245
I still can't deal with that haircut.

1204
01:38:37,329 --> 01:38:39,414
I wouldn't even
recognize you on the street.

1205
01:38:39,498 --> 01:38:41,249
Well, I'm a new man.

1206
01:38:42,584 --> 01:38:43,585
I've already ordered.

1207
01:38:59,518 --> 01:39:01,103
What?

1208
01:45:32,744 --> 01:45:33,745
ENGLISH US - PSDH
